#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d8c6c93-9d15-5482-8130-25bb9cabf008" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions to Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b521aa6-ad34-5bd0-a027-7e15aa3917a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269332} {:answer/id #uuid "2fea7e4f-7e88-5573-acd5-0c95f164f92b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (10) − 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269333} {:answer/id #uuid "b550f014-d9a9-57f7-9be6-01dc4c38f946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2 − \\ln (10))/(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269334} {:answer/id #uuid "e3fd7bd9-f273-5745-bf27-5e3e6bfa46d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 − \\ln (10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269335} {:answer/id #uuid "6eed126e-9264-594f-b0fd-7c559932d3ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 − 3\\ln (10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b521aa6-ad34-5bd0-a027-7e15aa3917a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269332} :answer-field/id #uuid "23b9891e-99de-525b-9260-c2d746e66ec9" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274141}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "272af2d8-c0a8-526a-8af7-4492ae771147" :question/math-academy-id "q-100282" :question/problem "Find the exact solution of the equation ${e}^{t}=11.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & =11 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}({e}^{t}) & =\\operatorname{ln}(11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nt=\\operatorname{ln}(11)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $t=\\operatorname{ln}(11).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91bf0b47-327f-5b82-a87a-307b7eda9e1c" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Arithmetic Series Using Sigma Notation when Not All Terms are Shown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed3f3e66-7f9a-5b48-8cfc-a4ff2eceb591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{9}(\\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}n)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269657} {:answer/id #uuid "6f5dba87-060e-58e3-b7e1-796760241737" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{9}(\\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}n)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269658} {:answer/id #uuid "23266f91-002c-5378-a3f1-4c6bc0b20da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{9}(\\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}n)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269659} {:answer/id #uuid "d56c56d8-abc2-58fb-84fe-bc702b72c77f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac12\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{9}(\\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}n)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269660} {:answer/id #uuid "0645b2f8-8f91-5d4c-801c-2d56ab9bc5a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{9}(\\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0645b2f8-8f91-5d4c-801c-2d56ab9bc5a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{9}(\\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269661} :answer-field/id #uuid "49386536-2e58-528f-9cc1-0ca6f425d31b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274206}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4f95e576-a69f-50d3-a4ad-5ca5441cb0c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-107659" :question/problem "Express the following arithmetic series using sigma notation.\n\n$$\n2+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{3}{2}+…-\\frac{41}{4}-12\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term ${a}_{1}=2$ and the common difference is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =2+(n-1)(-\\frac{7}{4}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{7}{4}n+\\frac{7}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe don't know the index of the last term, but we can find it using the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-12 \\\\ \\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}n & =-12 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4}n & =\\frac{15}{4}+12 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4}n & =\\frac{63}{4} \\\\ n & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are $9$ terms in total, from $n=1$ to $n=9.$ So, the sigma notation for the given sum is:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{9}(\\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}n)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91bf0b47-327f-5b82-a87a-307b7eda9e1c" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Arithmetic Series Using Sigma Notation when Not All Terms are Shown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e1f98c5-4dc8-540b-92ff-eb27b64fa8ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac12\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{17}(-7n+3)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269662} {:answer/id #uuid "5615e7de-d47f-55c4-bea0-15d9bd28a519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{17}(-7n+3)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269663} {:answer/id #uuid "c0793ceb-5f1d-5903-a766-fce011958284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{17}(-7n+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269664} {:answer/id #uuid "e1beed13-783e-5574-ac48-6d2ea0deca78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{17}(-7n+3)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269665} {:answer/id #uuid "575613cc-cc2c-555b-ab66-085145462f07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{17}(-7n+3)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0793ceb-5f1d-5903-a766-fce011958284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{17}(-7n+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269664} :answer-field/id #uuid "536d7ed3-929b-58df-88ca-9c305dcfb9d9" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274207}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "114b36be-4de0-5cb7-978d-00c318e1a7d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-107668" :question/problem "Express the following arithmetic series using sigma notation.\n\n$$\n-4-11-18+…-109-116\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term ${a}_{1}=-4$ and the common difference is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =-11-(-4) \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-4+(n-1)(-7) \\\\  & =-4-7n+7 \\\\  & =-7n+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe don't know the index of the last term, but we can find it using the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-116 \\\\ -7n+3 & =-116 \\\\ -7n & =-119 \\\\ n & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are $17$ terms in total, from $n=1$ to $n=17.$ So, the sigma notation for the given sum is:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{17}(-7n+3)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256745 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "829ac8f1-7ade-5dd9-9f2f-78234a57a257" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum of an Arithmetic Series Given the First and Last Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ca6ddb5-9048-5d3b-9727-372541600294" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "138" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269672} {:answer/id #uuid "612e881d-2ce2-53b3-9e32-ca6ee39a8c9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "142" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269673} {:answer/id #uuid "33a266ae-6a26-5326-b8d9-8ff998f020f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "616" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269674} {:answer/id #uuid "e07acdee-af8e-5117-9f1f-c8137efddbc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "156" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269675} {:answer/id #uuid "7bfddea5-4592-56af-a9cf-9cac00ef90ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33a266ae-6a26-5326-b8d9-8ff998f020f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "616" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269674} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f8639ee-bbd7-5e3b-a108-462ae4037009" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274209}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c01979e1-2f0d-57eb-bf22-2ecf15ad2571" :question/math-academy-id "q-107749" :question/problem "Find the sum of the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n5+11+17+23+⋯+83\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{N}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, ${a}_{N}$ is the last term, and $N$ is the total number of terms.\n\nFor our series, we have ${a}_{1}=5$ and the common difference is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =11-5 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =5+(n-1)(6) \\\\  & =5+6n-6 \\\\  & =6n-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we know, the last term is ${a}_{N}=83.$ Putting this value in the formula for the $n$th term obtained above, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{N} & =6N-1 \\\\ 83 & =6N-1 \\\\ 84 & =6N \\\\ N & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting ${a}_{1}=5$, ${a}_{14}=83$, and $N=14$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{14} & =\\frac{14}{2}(5+83) \\\\  & =7\\cdot 88 \\\\  & =616\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256748 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "829ac8f1-7ade-5dd9-9f2f-78234a57a257" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum of an Arithmetic Series Given the First and Last Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e722c290-1ee5-5467-ad86-6d197deea7cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "138" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269677} {:answer/id #uuid "229b30b2-6ce3-5603-bc16-b61ebd605056" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "142" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269678} {:answer/id #uuid "da0e5262-6295-5c3e-9bb5-78202611a9e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\,364" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269679} {:answer/id #uuid "c292f3e5-c6c2-58e9-adb2-b96056f7f111" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "156" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269680} {:answer/id #uuid "f179ed8e-2fd7-58ba-babb-6048f4ca98ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da0e5262-6295-5c3e-9bb5-78202611a9e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\,364" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269679} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5bea798-e393-51c8-bb2b-12b4a8be0441" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274210}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06cac020-1582-549c-9faf-4dee975a31ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-107751" :question/problem "Find the sum of the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n(-14)+(-20)+(-26)+(-32)+⋯+(-314)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{N}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, ${a}_{N}$ is the last term, and $N$ is the total number of terms.\n\nFor our series, we have ${a}_{1}=-14$ and the common difference is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =-20-(-14) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-14+(n-1)(-6) \\\\  & =-14-6n+6 \\\\  & =-6n-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we know, the last term is ${a}_{N}=-314.$ Putting this value in the formula for the $n$th term obtained above, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{N} & =-6N-8 \\\\ -314 & =-6N-8 \\\\ -306 & =-6N \\\\ N & =51\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting ${a}_{1}=-14$, ${a}_{51}=-314$, and $N=51$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{51} & =\\frac{N}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{N}) \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{2}\\cdot (-14-314) \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{2}\\cdot (-328) \\\\  & =-8\\,364\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256751 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d65ee5e-2211-5822-a99a-c49dfe4d2bbc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum of an Arithmetic Series Given the First Term and the Total Number of Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5d31dd9-877e-536c-9b48-5c8237167c55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,020" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269682} {:answer/id #uuid "1cfeb52f-0699-50e1-9203-2de6d4b450c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "460" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269683} {:answer/id #uuid "f0f837b8-18cb-54d0-94a3-737b293e8f5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "448" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269684} {:answer/id #uuid "7a031650-d844-5df4-b193-9c3e582fd658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "452" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269685} {:answer/id #uuid "5cbf0ab9-2567-5147-a613-d5e83ce321ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "455" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5d31dd9-877e-536c-9b48-5c8237167c55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,020" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269682} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1699b8b-64f3-53e8-89ee-617a697ed776" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274211}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34c81d61-3e3f-5632-9eae-10ea56529564" :question/math-academy-id "q-107760" :question/problem "Calculate the sum of the first $20$ terms of the following arithmetic series. \n\n$$\n13+17+21+⋯\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nWe are given the total number of terms $N=20$ and the first term is ${a}_{1}=13.$ Now, the common difference $d$ can be found as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =17-13 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${a}_{1}=13$, $d=4$, and $N=20$ into the above formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{20} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{2}(2\\cdot 13+(20-1)\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{2}\\cdot (26+76) \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{2}\\cdot 102 \\\\  & =10\\cdot 102 \\\\  & =1\\,020\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256754 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d65ee5e-2211-5822-a99a-c49dfe4d2bbc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum of an Arithmetic Series Given the First Term and the Total Number of Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05d519a2-8690-578d-bb9f-ed628f3132df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "460" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269687} {:answer/id #uuid "5c79eb3d-1fc0-539b-ac13-fe86dbcba0d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "448" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269688} {:answer/id #uuid "4ce8d8d8-fb17-554e-b6cf-68f14b72e270" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "452" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269689} {:answer/id #uuid "d5d69aec-efd4-5732-94f0-e83e0d7548f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "455" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269690} {:answer/id #uuid "5239979c-9e81-5292-997a-17d2ef949ea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5239979c-9e81-5292-997a-17d2ef949ea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269691} :answer-field/id #uuid "8179a8f6-98ea-5dab-98f6-f45110dfd8e7" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274212}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e928ff1c-3648-56d1-a33b-01de56cefa49" :question/math-academy-id "q-107794" :question/problem "Calculate the sum of the first $15$ terms of the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n-1+(-\\frac{2}{5})+\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}+⋯\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nWe are given the total number of terms $N=15$ and the first term is ${a}_{1}=-1.$ Now, the common difference $d$ can be found as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}-(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${a}_{1}=-1$, $d=\\frac{3}{5}$, and $N=15$ into the above formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{15} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}(2(-1)+(15-1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot (-2+8.4) \\\\  & =7.5\\cdot 6.4 \\\\  & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256789 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fa5bdf9-4b27-5565-9ee1-ecb9f9db4791" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Units of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f82bc5c6-3bda-5a06-a880-5062f89aa168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Visitors per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269732} {:answer/id #uuid "cceb913a-4934-56a6-9de2-7b3ad89c7589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Visitors" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269733} {:answer/id #uuid "8324b498-8f0a-50eb-be09-2df9a3792979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Hours per visitor" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269734} {:answer/id #uuid "5a09626c-2f3c-51c4-8093-7022348e3f00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Visitors times hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f82bc5c6-3bda-5a06-a880-5062f89aa168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Visitors per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269732} :answer-field/id #uuid "40677bcf-15fd-565c-bc4b-c201aefa03dc" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274221}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c773c57f-ca50-5bf6-b884-84eac176a3dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-110040" :question/problem "A website receives $v(h)$ visits every $h$ hours. What are the units of $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}h}?$" :question/worked-solution "We have that the time $h$ is measured in hours and the number of visits is measured in visitors. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h: & \\text{hours} \\\\ v(h): & \\text{visitors}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}h}$ measures a change in $v$ over a change in $h.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}h}: & \\frac{\\text{visitors}}{\\text{hours}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}h}$ is measured in \"visitors per hour \"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256803 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "655f4b04-4eec-5386-b349-7c26d6c6c586" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Geometric Series Using Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "730e37f0-c74a-58ff-99ad-fcc0ed4e31ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac12\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}{5}^{n-1}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269751} {:answer/id #uuid "c683a7ca-7d78-5e36-a638-ef44407ac045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}{5}^{n-1}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269752} {:answer/id #uuid "4ac94973-5748-599e-a5b1-21953663a1e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}{5}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269753} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf74bc0-2756-50a3-bcb6-31e02f6c7af7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}{5}^{n-1}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269754} {:answer/id #uuid "20832e04-066b-5985-ad41-d663c79a47de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}{5}^{n-1}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ac94973-5748-599e-a5b1-21953663a1e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}{5}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269753} :answer-field/id #uuid "db42fb6c-d552-5c42-9aa2-2c7c4239f19d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274225}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dbfdc4aa-e0a1-5cb8-a5f8-802705527624" :question/math-academy-id "q-110195" :question/problem "The geometric series $1+5+25+125+625$ can be written using sigma notation as" :question/worked-solution "We must express the given series in the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ denotes the first term of the series, $r$ denotes the common ratio, and $N$ denotes the number of terms in the series. In this case, ${a}_{1}=1$ and $N=5.$\n\nTo find $r,$ we calculate the ratio of the first two terms in the series:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{5}{1}=5\n$$\n\nTherefore, expressing the geometric series given in sigma notation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+5+25+125+625 & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}1\\cdot {5}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}{5}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "655f4b04-4eec-5386-b349-7c26d6c6c586" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Geometric Series Using Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b5a8f1b-bd01-5854-894d-2735366b9bf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}2\\cdot {4}^{n-1}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269756} {:answer/id #uuid "200fe113-f24d-5f10-8c56-ac1229c5756e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac12\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}2\\cdot {4}^{n-1}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269757} {:answer/id #uuid "5232a7c1-9419-55bc-8085-bb3c5cbf31c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}2\\cdot {4}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269758} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d60f67-769c-5659-b2ed-85ec8c3d8a85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}2\\cdot {4}^{n-1}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269759} {:answer/id #uuid "e08f94b6-e9e5-5fcc-b373-934fd559170e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}2\\cdot {4}^{n-1}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5232a7c1-9419-55bc-8085-bb3c5cbf31c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}2\\cdot {4}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269758} :answer-field/id #uuid "72bdfc64-e430-54ce-8238-6fb27fe57cc0" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274226}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8349b03f-f80a-5400-be8a-3eaa57417e1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-110200" :question/problem "The geometric series $2+8+32+128$ can be written using sigma notation as" :question/worked-solution "We must express the given series in the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ denotes the first term of the series, $r$ denotes the common ratio, and $N$ denotes the number of terms in the series. In this case, ${a}_{1}=2$ and $N=4.$\n\nTo find $r,$ we calculate the ratio of the first two terms in the series:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{8}{2}=4\n$$\n\nTherefore, expressing the geometric series given in sigma notation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+8+32+128 & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}2\\cdot {4}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57e44759-4db8-57e3-bfb4-ce026d4d73b2" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Geometric Series Using Sigma Notation by Calculating the Number of Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fac48c3a-e2cf-5a50-8b14-08cb816d20f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}2\\,401\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n-1}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269761} {:answer/id #uuid "8bcfd4db-d0bd-5597-b885-e897ca2b4839" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}2\\,401\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n-1}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269762} {:answer/id #uuid "14efbcda-ce43-5276-b90e-46ace5b8e0c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}2\\,401\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269763} {:answer/id #uuid "fabedba4-8c2f-5e94-ab6d-e2dc57ea9e32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac12\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}2\\,401\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n-1}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269764} {:answer/id #uuid "7f03d74a-9f06-52fa-b035-427f5fd86a93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}2\\,401\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n-1}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14efbcda-ce43-5276-b90e-46ace5b8e0c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}2\\,401\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269763} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0ef2f73-b703-5fa1-a5c5-230b612ca34b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274227}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "24124fc9-7f50-533d-9cb7-ef782b3922cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-110212" :question/problem "The geometric series $2\\,401+343+⋯+7$  can be expressed using sigma notation as" :question/worked-solution "We must express the given series in the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ denotes the first term of the series, $r$ denotes the common ratio, and $N$ denotes the number of terms in the series. In this case, ${a}_{1}=2\\,401.$\n\nTo find $r,$ we find the ratio of the consecutive terms ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{343}{2\\,401}=\\frac{1}{7}\n$$\n\nNow we must determine $N$ (the index of the last term). To do this, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n{a}_{N}={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{N-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{N} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{N-1} \\\\ 7 & =2\\,401\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{N-1} \\\\ \\frac{7}{2\\,401} & ={(\\frac{1}{7})}^{N-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{343} & =\\frac{1}{{7}^{N-1}} \\\\ 343 & ={7}^{N-1} \\\\ {7}^{3} & ={7}^{N-1} \\\\ 3 & =N-1 \\\\ N & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing the geometric series in sigma notation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\,401+343+⋯+7 & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}2\\,401\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd612e5f-cc72-5df4-94da-e290473ed0a4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum of a Geometric Series Given With a Zero Starting Index"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "750fbfbe-c93a-5786-a881-d7c63f24b00f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.777" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269781} {:answer/id #uuid "6edc47eb-69e8-5267-8531-813d74c45ab3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{242}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269782} {:answer/id #uuid "8a04d8bf-33e3-5040-af38-a3c0e89f9b12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.814" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269783} {:answer/id #uuid "cc4ff166-f245-5cf4-bf1e-0983597bb698" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.982" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269784} {:answer/id #uuid "9a9e59cf-c678-524d-a198-927526f9946d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.678" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6edc47eb-69e8-5267-8531-813d74c45ab3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{242}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269782} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c7e61e9-177d-5d8d-8ad4-21caa0f5f8a5" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274231}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "032226b5-f50b-5569-a40d-8825ca8a0e5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-110335" :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{4}2{(3)}^{n-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the given series is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. Notice that the index starts from $n=0$ instead of $n=1.$\n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{0}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=3.$ Also, note that $N=5,$ since there are $5$ terms in total.\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=2{(3)}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nand so the first term ${a}_{0}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =2{(3)}^{0-1} \\\\  & =2{(3)}^{-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{3},r=3$ and $N=5$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{5} & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{3}^{5})}{(1-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{3}^{5})}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-242) \\\\  & =\\frac{242}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb183013-a42d-50a3-a1b8-cd8501781075" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Polynomial With Multiple Variables as a Difference of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9b769a8-a67a-5298-9b03-306295fb14cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(({r}^{2}+25{s}^{2})(r+5s)(r-5s))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269835} {:answer/id #uuid "1d73a3c1-7256-5c51-8fa3-5e307db38688" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(({r}^{2}+25{s}^{2})(r+5s)(r-5s))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269836} {:answer/id #uuid "2510d168-a64e-5ba6-bc9a-e2edd3feeebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({r}^{2}+25{s}^{2})(r+5s)(r-5s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269837} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d08dc1-1edb-5038-8cd7-3f12fa22f71a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(({r}^{2}+25{s}^{2})(r+5s)(r-5s))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269838} {:answer/id #uuid "84d17b28-a8c1-5d5e-8167-1ad55b9cdb39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(({r}^{2}+25{s}^{2})(r+5s)(r-5s))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2510d168-a64e-5ba6-bc9a-e2edd3feeebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({r}^{2}+25{s}^{2})(r+5s)(r-5s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269837} :answer-field/id #uuid "09776543-3f23-575f-9b62-3947834868c7" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274242}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79c6e0be-06e2-559d-80d7-97545e89753f" :question/math-academy-id "q-112042" :question/problem "Factor the expression ${r}^{4}-(5s)^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n{r}^{4}-(5s)^{4}={({r}^{2})}^{2}-{((5s)^{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({r}^{2})}^{2}-{((5s)^{2})}^{2} & =({r}^{2}+(5s)^{2})({r}^{2}-(5s)^{2}) \\\\  & =({r}^{2}+25{s}^{2})({r}^{2}-(5s)^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares. Therefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({r}^{2}+25{s}^{2})({r}^{2}-(5s)^{2}) & =({r}^{2}+25{s}^{2})(r+5s)(r-5s)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb183013-a42d-50a3-a1b8-cd8501781075" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Polynomial With Multiple Variables as a Difference of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd247513-c820-56ca-8476-15eafd169a7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({j}^{2}+9{k}^{2})(j+3k)(j-3k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269845} {:answer/id #uuid "2398d0b7-0b3c-52fa-b8d7-ff9418746d96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(({j}^{2}+9{k}^{2})(j+3k)(j-3k))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269846} {:answer/id #uuid "ceba7b3d-ac5f-5094-9116-3001749098f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(({j}^{2}+9{k}^{2})(j+3k)(j-3k))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269847} {:answer/id #uuid "2055ac62-09e5-5c42-abc6-234346377d40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(({j}^{2}+9{k}^{2})(j+3k)(j-3k))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269848} {:answer/id #uuid "7af2ce74-1b88-5544-a921-ab2a81eab3aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(({j}^{2}+9{k}^{2})(j+3k)(j-3k))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd247513-c820-56ca-8476-15eafd169a7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({j}^{2}+9{k}^{2})(j+3k)(j-3k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269845} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0064f8c-4955-514f-b417-c9f6828e01a3" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274244}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9eff020-73a6-55b5-a5ce-93f7544df575" :question/math-academy-id "q-112045" :question/problem "Factor the expression ${j}^{4}-81{k}^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n{j}^{4}-81{k}^{4}={({j}^{2})}^{2}-{(9{k}^{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{({j}^{2})}^{2}-{(9{k}^{2})}^{2}=({j}^{2}+9{k}^{2})({j}^{2}-9{k}^{2})\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares:\n \n\n$$\n{j}^{2}-9{k}^{2}={j}^{2}-{(3k)}^{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({j}^{2}+9{k}^{2})({j}^{2}-9{k}^{2}) & =({j}^{2}+9{k}^{2})(j+3k)(j-3k)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7df31c9-b27a-5ce2-93c8-178ba96da000" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3365f1a4-8df5-518b-b47f-5dd14429aaff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(27)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269850} {:answer/id #uuid "faaa81bd-4cf8-5ab6-8a6c-abf848259ed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(27)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269851} {:answer/id #uuid "edf27920-07c5-5002-a7bc-b2a667322b88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(27)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269852} {:answer/id #uuid "0f4e9024-1933-50a0-b153-164862bc4400" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269853} {:answer/id #uuid "c3678e9b-ffa3-5cdc-9314-94273e0f97d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(27)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f4e9024-1933-50a0-b153-164862bc4400" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269853} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa5035c0-ddca-574e-b52f-5996ece6e72e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274245}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a6d442f-4688-5203-a723-98f8e649492f" :question/math-academy-id "q-112382" :question/problem "After taking a pill containing $300$ milligrams of a particular drug, the quantity of the drug in a patient's body decreases at a rate of $c(t)=300\\cdot {2}^{-0.7t}$ milligrams per hour, where $t$ is the time, in hours, since the patient consumed the pill. What will be the rate of decrease after $5$ hours? Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "Since we want to determine the rate of decrease in the quantity of the drug in the patient's body after $5$ hours, we have $t=5.$\n\nThus, the rate of decrease in the quantity of the drug after $5$ hours will be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c(5) & =300\\cdot {2}^{-0.7(5)} \\\\  & =300\\cdot {2}^{-3.5} \\\\  & \\approx 27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of decrease will be $27$ milligrams per hour."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256866 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa38f112-466c-50e9-ac08-be72b6f53782" :knowledge-point/title "Relating a Derivative to a Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "172cf3d7-e5f6-5a3d-9234-bd8b39ba2a9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H'(t)=2(r(t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269855} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca1cdd6-25ce-50df-b5fe-8f9aba1d34ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H'(t)=-(r(t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269856} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ae1ec2-f627-5edf-9b65-1eff2fff1335" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H'(t)=\\frac{1}{2}(r(t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269857} {:answer/id #uuid "adeef628-7c98-5a0a-8c8e-a305cba8308b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H'(t)=3(r(t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269858} {:answer/id #uuid "40138730-bd65-5afb-a675-38522747ee92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H'(t)=r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40138730-bd65-5afb-a675-38522747ee92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H'(t)=r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269859} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ea39c97-dcfa-5e71-b8c9-cd3cbf34f479" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274246}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f4c5624-08a5-5d28-9dbb-7ddefb779501" :question/math-academy-id "q-112509" :question/problem "Joseph is measuring the height reached by his lime tree by recording the following two numbers for different values of $t,$ where $t$ is the time, in years, since he planted the tree:\n\n- $H(t)$ - The tree's height, in centimeters\n\n- $r(t)$ - the rate at which the tree's height is increasing.\n\nWhat is the relationship between $H(t)$ and $r(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $H(t)$ gives the tree's height after $t$ years. Then,\n\n$$\n{H}^{'}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}H(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }H}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${H}^{'}(t)$ gives the rate at which the height of the tree increases at time $t$, which is the same as $r(t).$ Therefore, the relationship between $H(t)$ and $r(t)$ is ${H}^{'}(t)=r(t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256941 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f76bf587-59a0-5fd7-8c11-68d72c59856f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269959} {:answer/id #uuid "8edf8a99-e07e-5528-bfb7-d9432ea4544c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269960} {:answer/id #uuid "b56686ad-8250-5580-9ce9-cccc41725005" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269961} {:answer/id #uuid "8731e283-ab2c-5b57-8abe-fa9b14b5cdb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269962} {:answer/id #uuid "ba3584e1-2978-5993-b571-6f6ef3148ac1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2}(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f76bf587-59a0-5fd7-8c11-68d72c59856f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269959} :answer-field/id #uuid "49b1a99c-b820-5872-8341-954588e9bc3e" :answer-field/key "x" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274267}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c37cf0e8-bbca-5ef1-ad17-b5e71311507a" :question/math-academy-id "q-113523" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+3x-3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+3x-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+2)}{{x}^{2}(x-1)+3(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+2)}{({x}^{2}+3)(x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+3)(x-1)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x-1=0$ gives the solution $x=1.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+3=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256944 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bca4488-d692-5d27-8cb5-18665796febd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269964} {:answer/id #uuid "b8981010-cb18-5c5e-8223-9bdc372cff31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269965} {:answer/id #uuid "1fc8936e-91df-51a0-ba95-3ade8f5afc3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269966} {:answer/id #uuid "0c0160fa-7b70-531d-99e7-3f529b4d72d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bca4488-d692-5d27-8cb5-18665796febd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269964} :answer-field/id #uuid "84234d61-6f12-5415-84ee-79da697fec2d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274268}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89758aa9-2520-5f08-bcf3-4dd38de13609" :question/math-academy-id "q-113547" :question/problem "Find all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+x+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}(x+1)+(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+1}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x+1}}{\\cancel{(x+1)}({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ does not have any vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5803755a-e429-5cfc-9845-d5bba3869a88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269968} {:answer/id #uuid "200c2a42-afa0-5279-91ba-21141e324ea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269969} {:answer/id #uuid "02100587-8ec2-5817-a755-0191b034d7cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269970} {:answer/id #uuid "196379f9-ed31-5a72-bc23-59e849916de7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5803755a-e429-5cfc-9845-d5bba3869a88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269968} :answer-field/id #uuid "6792f074-6ddc-5608-812d-f8a86c3824f1" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274269}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c1d02b50-c109-51fe-8f01-39a10ce1834c" :question/math-academy-id "q-113550" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x}{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+x-3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x}{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+x-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(x-3)}{{x}^{2}(x-3)+(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(x-3)}{(x-3)({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cancel{(x-3)}}{\\cancel{(x-3)}({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7b17a61-b225-5493-98b0-58d15217aa31" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Cubic Equations by Grouping"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43ddc152-ca6d-5e85-95a3-2a13a39bd75d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\sqrt2)-1,\\sqrt2,3\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270137} {:answer/id #uuid "c30f63a9-f42e-54f5-8219-ce503c5254d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\sqrt2)-2,\\sqrt2,3\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270138} {:answer/id #uuid "f0bcf0df-cb99-5a29-a18f-cbc03aaeb2e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{-\\sqrt2,\\sqrt2,3\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270139} {:answer/id #uuid "295b826e-00c3-5deb-a50d-d4b5017cdd23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\sqrt2)+2,\\sqrt2,3\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270140} {:answer/id #uuid "af06cfc6-1029-5ceb-8d60-8aaf96ba1229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\sqrt2)+1,\\sqrt2,3\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0bcf0df-cb99-5a29-a18f-cbc03aaeb2e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{-\\sqrt2,\\sqrt2,3\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270139} :answer-field/id #uuid "6668fd33-a875-593b-b0ca-6b80d33b46ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274303}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4c3b57b-4f7f-5532-bd9e-228bcbf5fb45" :question/math-academy-id "q-114145" :question/problem "Solve ${m}^{3}-3{m}^{2}-2m+6=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{3}-3{m}^{2}-2m+6 & = \\\\ ({m}^{3}-3{m}^{2})-(2m-6) & = \\\\ {m}^{2}(m-3)-2(m-3) & = \\\\ (m-3)\\cdot {m}^{2}-(m-3)\\cdot 2 & = \\\\ (m-3)({m}^{2}-2) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(m-3)({m}^{2}-2)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(m-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m-3 & =0 \\\\ m & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({m}^{2}-2)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ {m}^{2} & =2 \\\\ m & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $m=3,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257066 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01d58dfd-315a-5a4f-ae16-1db04010169a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Cubic Equations With Negative Leading Coefficients by Grouping"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f7b46b8-60ef-5a95-9ad1-167a249588cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\frac72)+1,-\\frac13,\\frac13\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270142} {:answer/id #uuid "1ef784af-153f-5dc4-bde8-63dc98601485" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{-\\frac72,-\\frac13,\\frac13\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270143} {:answer/id #uuid "10bffa69-276e-52a5-903c-95eb6f4b885a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\frac72)+2,-\\frac13,\\frac13\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270144} {:answer/id #uuid "1f5255f4-4acc-5b34-84c4-997653454446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\frac72)-2,-\\frac13,\\frac13\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270145} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a34644-3283-53d2-be26-d1e1df0b865a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\frac72)-1,-\\frac13,\\frac13\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ef784af-153f-5dc4-bde8-63dc98601485" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{-\\frac72,-\\frac13,\\frac13\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270143} :answer-field/id #uuid "55cbc269-2e05-58ae-98b2-fb7a2ca12242" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274304}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3c48b1d-9c5d-5b27-89de-0584bf82834b" :question/math-academy-id "q-114205" :question/problem "Solve $-18{v}^{3}-63{v}^{2}+2v+7=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$ to make the coefficient of the cubic term positive. This gives\n\n$$\n18{v}^{3}+63{v}^{2}-2v-7=0.\n$$\n\nWe then proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18{v}^{3}+63{v}^{2}-2v-7 & = \\\\ 9{v}^{2}(2v+7)-1(2v+7) & = \\\\ (9{v}^{2}-1)(2v+7) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(9{v}^{2}-1)(2v+7)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(9{v}^{2}-1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{v}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{9} \\\\ v & =\\pm \\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(2v+7)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2v & =-7 \\\\ v & =-\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $v=-\\frac{7}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01d58dfd-315a-5a4f-ae16-1db04010169a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Cubic Equations With Negative Leading Coefficients by Grouping"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a20fa095-8e34-5519-8d09-3b707d5accd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-5)+3,-3,5\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270152} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f15bba-24d5-56f3-b50f-cedcab3cabff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-5)-1,-3,5\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270153} {:answer/id #uuid "7e2f3f2e-d51e-5874-94b8-53610ba16e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{-5,-3,5\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270154} {:answer/id #uuid "c4b1d8af-f6c2-5093-ba90-4e01e8abd2d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-5)+1,-3,5\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270155} {:answer/id #uuid "7ac48866-7b7d-58b1-a6dd-4cf9b32cf021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-5)-2,-3,5\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e2f3f2e-d51e-5874-94b8-53610ba16e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{-5,-3,5\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270154} :answer-field/id #uuid "87d5975f-83d5-5c2b-906c-0478dc07ac8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274306}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "62a71b17-2204-59c1-8d38-eaaccf24e868" :question/math-academy-id "q-114210" :question/problem "Solve $-{m}^{3}-3{m}^{2}+25m+75=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$ to make the coefficient of the cubic term positive. This gives\n\n$$\n{m}^{3}+3{m}^{2}-25m-75=0.\n$$\n\nWe then proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{3}+3{m}^{2}-25m-75 & = \\\\ ({m}^{3}+3{m}^{2})+(-25m-75) & = \\\\ {m}^{2}(m+3)-25(m+3) & = \\\\ ({m}^{2}-25)(m+3) & = \\\\ (m+5)(m-5)(m+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(m+5)(m-5)(m+3)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(m+5)$ equal to $0,$ we get $m=-5.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(m-5)$ equal to $0,$ we get $m=5.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(m+3)$ equal to $0,$ we get $m=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $m=-3,\\,\\pm 5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257086 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6158975-c81e-5621-aa39-3f2dc728f107" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Cubic Equations by Manipulating the Equation and Then Grouping"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "043bed87-b984-5e20-9fa0-223c7ed5d7db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{-3,\\frac34,3\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270162} {:answer/id #uuid "c35bed5a-e3bb-5e07-b8f0-f81f94f40376" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-3)+2,\\frac34,3\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270163} {:answer/id #uuid "6f1df551-c239-5e05-a798-f07dc42b888e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-3)-1,\\frac34,3\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270164} {:answer/id #uuid "b2cdbe6d-dcfb-530a-9470-43d8413ddf18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-3)+1,\\frac34,3\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270165} {:answer/id #uuid "737cc8ba-5578-570c-a444-238ee1af1d33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-3)-2,\\frac34,3\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "043bed87-b984-5e20-9fa0-223c7ed5d7db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{-3,\\frac34,3\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270162} :answer-field/id #uuid "63dc08fe-dd3a-519d-9a62-686cdc2b86dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274308}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a79ebad8-83ac-5875-bf1f-d77b87643be1" :question/math-academy-id "q-114339" :question/problem "Solve $8{u}^{3}-6{u}^{2}-72u+54=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we have an overall common factor of $2$, we start by dividing our equation through by $2.$ This gives \n\n$$\n4{u}^{3}-3{u}^{2}-36u+27=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we factor by grouping. Notice that when grouping the smaller terms, we factor out the negative sign.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{u}^{3}-3{u}^{2}-36u+27 & = \\\\ (4{u}^{3}-3{u}^{2})-(36u-27) & = \\\\ {u}^{2}(4u-3)-9(4u-3) & = \\\\ (4u-3)\\cdot {u}^{2}-(4u-3)\\cdot 9 & = \\\\ (4u-3)({u}^{2}-9) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set each factor equal to $0$ and solve:\n\n- Setting the factor $(4u-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4u-3 & =0 \\\\ 4u & =3 \\\\ u & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({u}^{2}-9)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ {u}^{2} & =9 \\\\ u & =\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the solutions to the given equation are $\\frac{3}{4}$ and $\\pm 3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dff5b7c-544a-5fda-a175-51ce4723a135" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a \"Greater Than or Equal To\" Inequality Given the Graph of the Corresponding Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36abfdbf-ab21-5c79-b85d-20155d5c5846" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270187} {:answer/id #uuid "3afb1e5c-2117-5bb3-8a27-004cf63eebfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,\\infty)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270188} {:answer/id #uuid "0af73d54-9964-5d7a-bbad-a075f2933d85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,-1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270189} {:answer/id #uuid "590276cd-5ca9-5ff6-8240-d7bd649121a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270190} {:answer/id #uuid "3eeb0847-6d2d-5dc7-8470-5db87d1addf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "590276cd-5ca9-5ff6-8240-d7bd649121a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270190} :answer-field/id #uuid "15d4f2a4-6f53-555f-8561-82e7163bd972" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274313}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a8e5c30-d271-53bb-8418-8317e11f8e2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-115324" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/8c266bd4087fbfad0f81de882dc72cf03e81d420897f1f1b1a3b430827a77f2a.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $(x+2)(x-2)\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n(x+2)(x-2)\\ge 0\n$$\n\nis a \"*greater than or equal to*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=(x+2)(x-2)\n$$\n\nis *above* the $x$-axis or *touches* this axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/1b6d9689cf4ac9d36507b4fc3ba0ea77ac34ee8a4cf5cd28a5592c834f48ecec.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x\\le -2$ or $x\\ge 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc018e0e-60bc-59bd-a3f6-8ae2b68f8cfb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality by Rearranging Terms and Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7e5a0c4-40a1-5387-b9c3-a8ad63edab7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270232} {:answer/id #uuid "ef25da5d-bdaa-54b3-ba31-47b237755561" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-1,5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270233} {:answer/id #uuid "70064126-21d1-525d-a06d-e1e414398902" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,\\infty)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270234} {:answer/id #uuid "e11e01a9-dad8-589a-8e7e-4e9d917a6311" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,-1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270235} {:answer/id #uuid "eaead088-de8e-5aba-add5-a9cb84c1a08a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef25da5d-bdaa-54b3-ba31-47b237755561" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-1,5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270233} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e7c94e0-dc77-5729-ad62-bb48e16b0153" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274322}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e171ae29-e8a4-5154-8cf5-f799b433e6ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-116199" :question/problem "Find the solution of $\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x\\le x+\\frac{5}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x & \\le x+\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-2x-\\frac{5}{2} & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}-4x-5) & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(x+1)(x-5) & \\le 0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-5) & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+1)(x-5)=0.$ The roots are $x=-1$ and $x=5,$ and they split the number line into $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-1),$ $(-1,5)$ and $(5,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+1)(x-5)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+1)(x-5)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+1)(x-5)$ is zero or negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-1,5).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-1$ and $x=5.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x\\le x+\\frac{5}{2}$ for all $x\\in [-1,5].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc018e0e-60bc-59bd-a3f6-8ae2b68f8cfb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality by Rearranging Terms and Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2775eee-d42d-552f-ae3e-a07c141ff855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270237} {:answer/id #uuid "c8b54629-9e9d-5d58-b51a-373cd218a857" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-5,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270238} {:answer/id #uuid "8a393f8b-a2ec-5b82-86ec-f77b2d814da6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,\\infty)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270239} {:answer/id #uuid "21d346f5-bfb2-504d-ab9f-13bafe62d017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,-1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270240} {:answer/id #uuid "e478ec3c-2a3e-5a93-95e7-39ec5236c9a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8b54629-9e9d-5d58-b51a-373cd218a857" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-5,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270238} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cfe6148-a9b9-506b-a141-b60e907939ff" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274323}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "31b9e724-be24-5c0d-8724-b3dd3a9b5157" :question/math-academy-id "q-116201" :question/problem "Find the solution of $\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{10}x\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{10}x & \\le 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{10}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{10}x-2 & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{10}({x}^{2}+x-20) & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{10}(x+5)(x-4) & \\le 0 \\\\ (x+5)(x-4) & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+5)(x-4)=0.$ The roots are $x=-5$ and $x=4,$ and they split the number line into $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-5),$ $(-5,4)$ and $(4,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+5)(x-4)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+5)(x-4)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+5)(x-4)$ is zero or negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-5,4).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-5$ and $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{10}x\\le 2$ for all $x\\in [-5,4].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257345 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "283415d8-47d7-5d42-8836-0c0b0c4efc85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Values Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47fd0df1-99c9-5258-b0e0-e15670b1a3bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270392} {:answer/id #uuid "f149c53a-9544-55af-a207-8ca4ca4645e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270393} {:answer/id #uuid "22f29766-0669-5bc5-b740-a816236acd6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270394} {:answer/id #uuid "383dd957-da7f-5a58-8db0-c6b0b541a454" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270395} {:answer/id #uuid "d551373f-7bfa-527b-acab-5cedad8ac939" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f149c53a-9544-55af-a207-8ca4ca4645e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270393} :answer-field/id #uuid "622a7410-d8b1-563f-83fb-4b7dc0409fd8" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274354}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dca028c6-39fc-5c90-b60c-c0a6062705b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-119481" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\operatorname{cos}(-{75}^{∘}).$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $-{75}^{∘}={60}^{∘}-{135}^{∘}.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(x-y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $x={60}^{∘}$ and $y={135}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(-{75}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}({60}^{∘}-{135}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}{60}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}{135}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}{60}^{∘}\\operatorname{sin}{135}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257389 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "143766e0-0ef5-5caf-b699-d394d4a707e9" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Products"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "245ad9ab-498a-57ce-9bc3-760ad172b223" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(1+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270422} {:answer/id #uuid "7e9a2bee-3054-56b2-bd80-f3dee1c7033d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(1+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270423} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf4f1b3-a3cb-5bed-bbe3-ffcd58cb2730" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(1+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270424} {:answer/id #uuid "3a098cc0-f790-5ed1-b077-d15de716bd26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270425} {:answer/id #uuid "aeacfcf3-19e8-5ae3-b35e-7fb6dc4163dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a098cc0-f790-5ed1-b077-d15de716bd26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270425} :answer-field/id #uuid "863845dd-1ea7-5ea7-a6cd-72f830a55671" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274360}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f30800d-77df-5a61-9a68-2afbf867dda1" :question/math-academy-id "q-119947" :question/problem "Simplify ${(\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parenthesis and use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+{cos}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =[{sin}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+{cos}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})]+2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =1+2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))}^{2}=1+2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f9683d9-05b9-5a42-9f81-0170a15dfac1" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Ratios"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7322eb6f-6592-54ac-bd45-3cb871ce3dc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\tan 70^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270427} {:answer/id #uuid "01be75a9-93ae-58b1-a94a-dbb12780fcda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sin 70^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270428} {:answer/id #uuid "f70a788d-5e90-580a-92de-a42378f67374" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270429} {:answer/id #uuid "2b32750c-e317-562f-85b8-19eda93ffaef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2)\\sin 70^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270430} {:answer/id #uuid "1aa412e8-c900-5676-ba38-7c0f78b4ae55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan 35^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f70a788d-5e90-580a-92de-a42378f67374" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270429} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ba8120d-dfd3-5be2-bbc7-54319393e1dd" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274361}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b566fa84-3938-5596-bfbc-9b6e5426b71c" :question/math-academy-id "q-120026" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}{80}^{∘}\\operatorname{tan}{40}^{∘}-1}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}2x=2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={40}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot {40}^{∘}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}({80}^{∘}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}{80}^{∘}\\operatorname{tan}{40}^{∘}-1}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}}-1}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{sin}^{2}{40}^{∘}-1}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}{40}^{∘}-1+{sin}^{2}{40}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}{40}^{∘}-(1-{sin}^{2}{40}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing  the Pythagorean identity in the form ${cos}^{2}{40}^{∘}=1-{sin}^{2}{40}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}{40}^{∘}-(1-{sin}^{2}{40}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}} & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}{40}^{∘}-{cos}^{2}{40}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘})(\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘})}(\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘})}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c898bbf-004c-5905-a0a0-761adefc8bd8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Value of a Trigonometric Expression Using the Double-Angle Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13d94a30-746a-55e9-a726-465aaa0106a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270432} {:answer/id #uuid "5e54d065-8787-5402-ba13-3b3fa9db3e1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270433} {:answer/id #uuid "1a53a3b4-a2a2-5e0f-9737-813f060852e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270434} {:answer/id #uuid "ada77982-d19d-55c8-ada3-9fe40b984d39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270435} {:answer/id #uuid "c5591592-bdef-5c79-9c73-9e798d8653d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13d94a30-746a-55e9-a726-465aaa0106a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270432} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2234ba0-5d29-5b80-bc72-06164d8e6cf5" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274362}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "766de5e3-4cf3-5599-8f12-d24d0a317252" :question/math-academy-id "q-120033" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c898bbf-004c-5905-a0a0-761adefc8bd8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Value of a Trigonometric Expression Using the Double-Angle Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a9d813f-1e76-5ed5-988d-adf18cd17d3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270437} {:answer/id #uuid "e0d2df05-c957-546b-a52d-4a7e00623af7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270438} {:answer/id #uuid "28dbed7a-bcd7-5305-b36d-6e9b63f30e1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270439} {:answer/id #uuid "ee1e98f6-86f5-5cd9-a838-8dd04bfb325d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270440} {:answer/id #uuid "a28fb4c7-7b72-5f7e-9bc2-83b1928400cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee1e98f6-86f5-5cd9-a838-8dd04bfb325d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270440} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce8834bd-c44d-55fe-beb6-a3fb814fd9a3" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274363}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d593e11-f74c-57da-a9e4-9637db488d53" :question/math-academy-id "q-120036" :question/problem "Find the exact value of ${(\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})-\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘}))}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parenthesis and use the Pythagorean identity.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})-\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘}))}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}({165}^{∘})-2\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘})+{cos}^{2}({165}^{∘}) \\\\  & =[{sin}^{2}({165}^{∘})+{cos}^{2}({165}^{∘})]-2\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1-2\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={165}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot {165}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}({330}^{∘}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n1-2\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘})=1-(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{(\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})+\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘}))}^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257404 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d820aa9f-7c02-53b5-9b09-c25ee59dbdaf" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula Multiple Times"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7af7bb71-1e34-55fd-935c-c7a3d3944fbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270447} {:answer/id #uuid "730ed948-9b33-553f-95be-94b7c864391c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270448} {:answer/id #uuid "39887de5-1684-5b6d-a5b2-17a4e7554937" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270449} {:answer/id #uuid "db1aa3e1-ba55-5688-a12c-a01cdbc4d991" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270450} {:answer/id #uuid "fadeca06-c455-560e-a0f8-7308261423e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "730ed948-9b33-553f-95be-94b7c864391c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270448} :answer-field/id #uuid "01b19cfc-a634-5c91-89df-e5c914ad047f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274365}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "07ab80ff-a6ba-5457-8088-8c6b6073aa0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-120094" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})=\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{16})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})=\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{8}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$, we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} & =\\frac{4}{2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7b17a61-b225-5493-98b0-58d15217aa31" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Cubic Equations by Grouping"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b15cb67c-3d63-577c-9916-312387d611d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-7)-2,-\\sqrt3,\\sqrt3\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270907} {:answer/id #uuid "dced93e7-dd59-58ac-9012-6072046cb8b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-7)+2,-\\sqrt3,\\sqrt3\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270908} {:answer/id #uuid "73ad5f7c-2ff4-5751-b436-fe9a072fa988" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-7)-1,-\\sqrt3,\\sqrt3\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270909} {:answer/id #uuid "5f7cf691-e0ef-5307-8856-10abdba0066f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-7)+1,-\\sqrt3,\\sqrt3\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270910} {:answer/id #uuid "bc5e5034-fb5c-589b-aca8-42ac31ace61c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{-7,-\\sqrt3,\\sqrt3\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc5e5034-fb5c-589b-aca8-42ac31ace61c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{-7,-\\sqrt3,\\sqrt3\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270911} :answer-field/id #uuid "baad478a-6b7e-5c37-a943-ba4d2c769f8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274457}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b72a04f0-5d72-598f-8e4e-47af21a1c978" :question/math-academy-id "q-14077" :question/problem "Solve ${x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-3x-21=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-3x-21 & = \\\\ ({x}^{3}+7{x}^{2})-(3x+21) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+7)-3(x+7) & = \\\\ (x+7)\\cdot {x}^{2}-(x+7)\\cdot 3 & = \\\\ (x+7)({x}^{2}-3) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(x+7)({x}^{2}-3)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(x+7)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+7 & =0 \\\\ x & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({x}^{2}-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-7,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6158975-c81e-5621-aa39-3f2dc728f107" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Cubic Equations by Manipulating the Equation and Then Grouping"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efe7eedd-a2ea-52c9-b73a-fa14b6357bfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\sqrt{\\frac12})+2,\\sqrt{\\frac12},\\frac43\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270917} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ca4d97-e30e-5e96-b936-f1f6f2e9332c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\sqrt{\\frac12})-1,\\sqrt{\\frac12},\\frac43\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270918} {:answer/id #uuid "2f44f6e7-8c93-52bd-9fce-0b823a911432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\sqrt{\\frac12})+1,\\sqrt{\\frac12},\\frac43\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270919} {:answer/id #uuid "ee3aa495-7dfa-5ad4-a272-e47cc6d4c4f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\sqrt{\\frac12})-2,\\sqrt{\\frac12},\\frac43\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270920} {:answer/id #uuid "404f61d1-cf9d-5db9-b6bd-a567e3ba672d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{-\\sqrt{\\frac12},\\sqrt{\\frac12},\\frac43\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "404f61d1-cf9d-5db9-b6bd-a567e3ba672d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{-\\sqrt{\\frac12},\\sqrt{\\frac12},\\frac43\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270921} :answer-field/id #uuid "25cb301f-8196-5531-bd43-8e75f93e84f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274459}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23a22035-6f86-5f86-abdd-41c57b6c7a46" :question/math-academy-id "q-14102" :question/problem "Solve $6{y}^{3}+4=8{y}^{2}+3y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we subtract $8{y}^{2}$ and $3y$ from both sides to make the right side $0.$ To prepare for factoring by grouping, we make sure to write the resulting cubic in standard form.\n\n$$\n6{y}^{3}-8{y}^{2}-3y+4=0\n$$\n\nNext, we factor by grouping. Notice that when grouping the smaller terms, we factor out the negative sign.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{y}^{3}-8{y}^{2}-3y+4 & = \\\\ (6{y}^{3}-8{y}^{2})-(3y-4) & = \\\\ 2{y}^{2}(3y-4)-(3y-4) & = \\\\ (3y-4)\\cdot 2{y}^{2}-(3y-4)\\cdot 1 & = \\\\ (3y-4)(2{y}^{2}-1) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set each factor equal to $0$ and solve.\n\n- Setting the factor $(3y-4)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y-4 & =0 \\\\ 3y & =4 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(2{y}^{2}-1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=\\frac{4}{3},\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "385b1ccd-ea68-5c59-a0e6-1a0bb7937460" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Cubic Equations With Leading Coefficients by Grouping"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b74e695-c2f4-57f6-8b4a-49806da2d032" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\sqrt{\\frac37})-1,\\frac12,\\sqrt{\\frac37}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270922} {:answer/id #uuid "f01ef6cf-a01c-55c5-a2be-16f9d109e47f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\sqrt{\\frac37})+1,\\frac12,\\sqrt{\\frac37}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270923} {:answer/id #uuid "ecc85ecd-b561-5922-afb1-ea15cf928a36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\sqrt{\\frac37})-2,\\frac12,\\sqrt{\\frac37}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270924} {:answer/id #uuid "c308c1d4-0373-54a8-9642-e4d468b1b79a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{-\\sqrt{\\frac37},\\frac12,\\sqrt{\\frac37}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270925} {:answer/id #uuid "e75454c4-5efa-578c-a839-53b5872c1064" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\sqrt{\\frac37})+2,\\frac12,\\sqrt{\\frac37}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c308c1d4-0373-54a8-9642-e4d468b1b79a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{-\\sqrt{\\frac37},\\frac12,\\sqrt{\\frac37}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270925} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c7a7495-9581-5a8a-8d87-9491d1bfb7a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274460}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a40984bf-4ba0-5fcb-b3fc-f4dcbc8bd041" :question/math-academy-id "q-14108" :question/problem "Solve $14{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}-6x+3=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed to factor the left side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}-6x+3 & = \\\\ (14{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2})-(6x-3) & = \\\\ 7{x}^{2}(2x-1)-3(2x-1) & = \\\\ (2x-1)\\cdot 7{x}^{2}-(2x-1)\\cdot 3 & = \\\\ (2x-1)(7{x}^{2}-3) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(2x-1)(7{x}^{2}-3)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(2x-1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(7{x}^{2}-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{x}^{2}-3 & =0 \\\\ 7{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{7}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{7}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258084 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36d4bf37-4818-58ac-9e39-75ce8ceef06a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a \"Less Than or Equal To\" Inequality Given the Graph of the Corresponding Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ff9b548-0073-5fe3-9de6-c912d28d1054" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270927} {:answer/id #uuid "d3f3827c-b501-59d0-85a2-402e73c37251" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270928} {:answer/id #uuid "b9099cd4-f3d8-5c60-af14-0adf9842ece3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,\\infty)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270929} {:answer/id #uuid "f2507a4a-0252-5b93-9dad-4239f182d811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,-1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270930} {:answer/id #uuid "9b32d40a-4085-559d-8406-be66651bd2e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3f3827c-b501-59d0-85a2-402e73c37251" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270928} :answer-field/id #uuid "b301a3ef-d0a5-5c41-9df0-d340f58c37ac" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274461}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dee958e0-6ef4-559d-9402-576ab9209618" :question/math-academy-id "q-141106" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/b70f78004ad1239cc6d7fb46086f8b961d587e69ff5c3a7414beba60cbcf74a9.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $-x(x+6)\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n-x(x+6)\\le 0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*less than or equal to*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-x(x+6)\n$$\n\nis *below* the $x$-axis or *touches* this axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ed78f461be7f5627814b8df3a85bedcbafbb1456c2dda01e825b0706d164b685.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x\\le -6$ or $x\\ge 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258087 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "385b1ccd-ea68-5c59-a0e6-1a0bb7937460" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Cubic Equations With Leading Coefficients by Grouping"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36db4e30-4aa9-5b6b-a132-4b82f6b84654" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(\\frac{1}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270932} {:answer/id #uuid "aa51a876-f004-54ca-9fd5-8eb77c7b6326" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270933} {:answer/id #uuid "8d25e4b3-5739-5926-8a2a-5ed1260133d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\frac{1}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270934} {:answer/id #uuid "c3f90fa2-b730-51f5-8567-4519e34d3f19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(\\frac{1}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270935} {:answer/id #uuid "bf2633b1-9a80-5f78-96dc-1508a58e7948" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf2633b1-9a80-5f78-96dc-1508a58e7948" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270936} :answer-field/id #uuid "702010b9-d6f5-5b83-9092-da53e378526d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274462}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe52eb5f-7e5b-51d0-b560-47f98d7ed314" :question/math-academy-id "q-14121" :question/problem "Find all the real solutions to the equation $3{z}^{3}-{z}^{2}+27z-9=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{3}-{z}^{2}+27z-9 & = \\\\ (3{z}^{3}-{z}^{2})+(27z-9) & = \\\\ {z}^{2}(3z-1)+9(3z-1) & = \\\\ (3z-1)\\cdot {z}^{2}+(3z-1)\\cdot 9 & = \\\\ (3z-1)({z}^{2}+9) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(3z-1)({z}^{2}+9)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(3z-1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z-1 & =0 \\\\ 3z & =1 \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({z}^{2}+9)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince a squared real number cannot be negative, the equation ${z}^{2}+9=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore,  $z=\\frac{1}{3}$ is the only real solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258093 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5337e4-9289-52fc-ba01-a0e7a8df6285" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Polynomial as a Difference of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e5a7203-f52c-5636-a094-c4174e422189" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({n}^{2}+36)(n+6)(n-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270942} {:answer/id #uuid "0de74783-302a-54c4-906b-790859c26061" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(({n}^{2}+36)(n+6)(n-6))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270943} {:answer/id #uuid "bcffa9ec-ea61-5551-a930-bd3c58aee9f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(({n}^{2}+36)(n+6)(n-6))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270944} {:answer/id #uuid "95c89a2e-155d-5a6d-87ea-d6351246ba89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(({n}^{2}+36)(n+6)(n-6))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270945} {:answer/id #uuid "64d5cffe-31fa-5bf6-b1aa-7aec20bdac4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(({n}^{2}+36)(n+6)(n-6))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e5a7203-f52c-5636-a094-c4174e422189" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({n}^{2}+36)(n+6)(n-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270942} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ffcedbe-6b9b-5efd-8e30-431c9650770f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274464}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d866302b-c2d5-51c8-89dc-11e9ed67ca2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-14130" :question/problem "Factor ${n}^{4}-{36}^{2}$ completely." :question/worked-solution "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n{n}^{4}-{36}^{2}={({n}^{2})}^{2}-{36}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{({n}^{2})}^{2}-{36}^{2}=({n}^{2}+36)({n}^{2}-36)\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares. Therefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({n}^{2}+36)({n}^{2}-36) & =({n}^{2}+36)({n}^{2}-{6}^{2}) \\\\  & =({n}^{2}+36)(n+6)(n-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258102 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "831f9aa0-099e-5e0c-8a9d-c4828b629f64" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "759c2146-a214-5298-8bce-14efc523a4b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270957} {:answer/id #uuid "af5a8e99-f6ed-5f08-8c5c-0978928b1309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270958} {:answer/id #uuid "4e3cee6d-5c41-500a-bd78-b21d7be8b346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270959} {:answer/id #uuid "bc924b52-4c65-503e-8118-ccdd3e01f00d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(-\\operatorname{sin}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270960} {:answer/id #uuid "78fc4416-907a-519b-8a10-f7d9779aaf3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− \\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af5a8e99-f6ed-5f08-8c5c-0978928b1309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270958} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd4ddbe5-8d24-5dd5-8875-3a96aceb424f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274467}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4e30af5f-93af-52ef-810a-d66a6247d423" :question/math-academy-id "q-141380" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{cos}v-\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot 0-\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dff5b7c-544a-5fda-a175-51ce4723a135" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a \"Greater Than or Equal To\" Inequality Given the Graph of the Corresponding Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e00637db-fd22-51cc-a1d2-dc24a295bc90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271007} {:answer/id #uuid "924bf7f4-2510-5053-bd1c-8bba29e3fe3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\le x\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271008} {:answer/id #uuid "33c5d1c2-c05d-5c63-a61b-0ca575ce43c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,\\infty)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271009} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b255aa-bfb5-5291-8c39-0b3910f5b9ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,-1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271010} {:answer/id #uuid "47df158b-8c59-57f5-8649-7d55e3992fdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "924bf7f4-2510-5053-bd1c-8bba29e3fe3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\le x\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271008} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a2657ef-8194-5688-9f96-f5db9f693db6" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274477}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f22377a6-66a6-5350-ac0e-892b1407ed25" :question/math-academy-id "q-143365" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/469d229fba26a06ae27dd6014230124cc4b379c72d5cc8aa8b6ab4d026903eeb.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $-8+6x-{x}^{2}\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n-8+6x-{x}^{2}\\ge 0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*greater than or equal to*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-8+6x-{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nis *above* the $x$-axis or *touches* this axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/2929d99093fa5c658059ce0470e5d1e6fa293dcad3a8a5eff1aa83fc05d0a4c3.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $2\\le x\\le 4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc419917-fe34-5bf9-8bf0-1b022fce11ff" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a \"Less Than\" Inequality Given the Graph of the Corresponding Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f0598f0-0b4a-58bd-8cfb-acc713df0097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 3 < x < 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271022} {:answer/id #uuid "37e40e14-16fc-5a69-8182-5c1ec5cb8bd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0 < x < 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271023} {:answer/id #uuid "86d5bf49-9288-57db-ab62-53c247c43729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271024} {:answer/id #uuid "7bbc68c9-bc5b-5426-847e-2dc2955f7158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 2 < x < 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271025} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4a678f-871a-5163-a70a-f7e460f983dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 3 < x < 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86d5bf49-9288-57db-ab62-53c247c43729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271024} :answer-field/id #uuid "d75686ae-3e4a-52d2-92f0-338338b17ea3" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274480}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6488e91c-d0d1-5b12-999b-f3e91536690e" :question/math-academy-id "q-144415" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/89a11e09dcb47c6f445e4f7921e8d4670453b10b67be3921d5723728ee154381.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $3-2x-{x}^{2}<0.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n3-2x-{x}^{2}<0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*less than*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola  \n\n$$\ny=3-2x-{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nis *below* the $x$-axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/a73a7db40f0469ff37c14b0bf52a35f28b08ebf8224ab5b86c97f72d5d12aae1.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x<-3$ or    $x>1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb53971d-15be-579e-86f5-4fc8d0c10d6d" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Arithmetic Series Using Sigma Notation when All Terms are Shown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ab48e44-da10-5169-959f-9b59f223fce4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}(9n-8)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271235} {:answer/id #uuid "3035f302-6027-5472-8226-591f8df67da5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}(9n-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271236} {:answer/id #uuid "09d79d07-0d45-598e-9280-f841b295971d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}(9n-8)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271237} {:answer/id #uuid "fa4019b7-7575-5ef5-9a08-5446e553d6f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}(9n-8)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271238} {:answer/id #uuid "a36a5404-fae4-57b0-9630-1e66a9c5e9ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac12\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}(9n-8)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3035f302-6027-5472-8226-591f8df67da5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}(9n-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271236} :answer-field/id #uuid "c70308cb-846b-549d-9fcd-573ad3abf895" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274525}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0439465f-c44b-5ef9-840d-c708559f8d6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-17271" :question/problem "The arithmetic series $1+10+19+28+37+46$ can be written in sigma notation as" :question/worked-solution "The first term is ${a}_{1}=1$ and the common difference is: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =10-1 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =1+(n-1)(9) \\\\  & =1+9n-9 \\\\  & =9n-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are $6$ terms in total, from $n=1$ to $n=6.$ So the sigma notation for the given sum is:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}(9n-8)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258934 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "765ef63a-e3f0-503b-876d-3b4776b2cafe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Approximate Solutions to Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e388c577-7061-527f-99d6-695d6ff9b4ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.527" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e388c577-7061-527f-99d6-695d6ff9b4ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.527" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258936} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb92bf39-5411-5859-9598-c76250d50b5f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258935}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dce75f1f-4e44-5de1-9b3a-5c377c4f3af9" :question/math-academy-id "q-228899" :question/problem "If ${e}^{2x+3}=7,$ then $x\\approx ${{field-1}}, rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we take $ln$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x+3} & =7 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}({e}^{2x+3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}(7) \\\\ 2x+3 & =\\operatorname{ln}(7) \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{ln}(7)-3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(7)-3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx\\approx -0.527\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "765ef63a-e3f0-503b-876d-3b4776b2cafe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Approximate Solutions to Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5447b780-0ab4-56b4-8a53-e99d01a5e05f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5447b780-0ab4-56b4-8a53-e99d01a5e05f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259100} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad7f6d1f-e7da-5ee4-802b-8191318a5090" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259099}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d4fded30-6fdc-57c4-9ae4-833f7de89572" :question/math-academy-id "q-263084" :question/problem "If ${e}^{1-4x}=\\sqrt[3]{15},$ then $x\\approx ${{field-1}}, rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we take $ln$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-4x} & =\\sqrt[3]{15} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}({e}^{1-4x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}(\\sqrt[3]{15}) \\\\ 1-4x & =\\operatorname{ln}({15}^{1/3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule to the right-hand side and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-4x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}(15) \\\\ -4x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}(15)-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{ln}(15)+\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx\\approx 0.024\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd612e5f-cc72-5df4-94da-e290473ed0a4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum of a Geometric Series Given With a Zero Starting Index"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6eb5520-8d26-5f65-8501-73db7e4c1c5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{312}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6eb5520-8d26-5f65-8501-73db7e4c1c5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{312}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259120} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b0e809c-6844-5731-a64b-72f8fbdcd8bf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259119}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67a65dcf-aa63-52fc-b7e4-2c741bfe2157" :question/math-academy-id "q-269902" :question/problem "Evaluate the following geometric series:\n\n${\\sum }_{n=0}^{3}2{(5)}^{n-1}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since the given series is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. Notice that the index starts from $n=0$ instead of $n=1.$\n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{0}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=5.$ Also, note that $N=4,$ since there are $4$ terms in total.\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=2{(5)}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nand so the first term ${a}_{0}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =2{(5)}^{0-1} \\\\  & =2{(5)}^{-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{5},r=5$ and $N=4$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{4} & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{5}^{4})}{(1-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{5}^{4})}{(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1248}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{312}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259121 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3eec195c-6549-5de2-b3f0-0417e5f518e7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a \"Greater Than\" Inequality Given the Graph of the Corresponding Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36328637-9412-587e-a918-9062d6aeaf47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36328637-9412-587e-a918-9062d6aeaf47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259123} :answer-field/id #uuid "198d6e70-00b9-5595-b743-7bae5608c115" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259122} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55ee629e-2f4f-56da-923a-ed5c8ceb82f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55ee629e-2f4f-56da-923a-ed5c8ceb82f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259125} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f7cf7a2-a5f0-518e-a840-0d6a584a7028" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259124} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba5ac51f-011f-553c-9e36-c7254e9e2989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba5ac51f-011f-553c-9e36-c7254e9e2989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259127} :answer-field/id #uuid "9046ca6b-4916-52c7-b0a9-671b37cefacd" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259126}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72b318f0-d031-5113-b6de-a73c45f90d0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-270581" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/dab886380436be716e80864ac6f58503052c7962995ff65e7f6b0bc1a7a6e22e.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $(x-4)(x+2)>0.$\n\nThe solution to the inequality is $x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nor $x>$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n(x-4)(x+2)>0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*greater than*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola\n\n$$\ny=(x-4)(x+2)\n$$\n\n \nis *above* the $x$-axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/47aafc516c10a98a8302340bea8b0cc2239dad13cabfbe548e6e2cdadea2def6.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x<-2$ or $x>4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6a58962-cb06-519a-8c64-0dfa3b9c14d7" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Degrees Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49d8700b-9994-5907-ba2e-c4f20fa828df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49d8700b-9994-5907-ba2e-c4f20fa828df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259141} :answer-field/id #uuid "0412563f-3360-572e-b136-33ab483bc5e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259140}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9b6607b-b7a2-5915-bcf5-69205d886d0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-274499" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{cos}(x-{270}^{∘})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}x$ or $\\operatorname{cos}x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}(x-{270}^{∘})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{cos}v+\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v={270}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(x-{270}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}{270}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}{270}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot 0+\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259194 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60c99086-75e7-5d91-b425-4e1b6f582bcc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d5d60ca-ce6e-5818-80f6-5e1f00a4ea32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d5d60ca-ce6e-5818-80f6-5e1f00a4ea32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259196} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec5da6d3-c4f9-5015-b5e8-9cbbee98e21c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259195} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "983e13cd-944c-5cec-a2ca-63d865a191e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "983e13cd-944c-5cec-a2ca-63d865a191e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259198} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7d70ad1-2f32-53da-941b-6509c9c8c13c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259197}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af193db4-aced-5259-96b5-34c474664303" :question/math-academy-id "q-278249" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}(x-3)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(x-3)=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=0$ and $x=3.$ \n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=0$ and $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259199 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a186b166-928c-5bad-a996-3bdf6af419f5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b147d074-50c4-579f-b791-a85cf421ccbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b147d074-50c4-579f-b791-a85cf421ccbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259201} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f43ee4a-f94f-5cde-ad17-a3f76d1effde" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259200} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9142388-b025-5efb-95cb-753d76095add" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9142388-b025-5efb-95cb-753d76095add" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259203} :answer-field/id #uuid "d68d6d82-a6c8-5f79-977b-16b32614d59a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259202} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e7529f6-6980-56a6-af05-5ca35dd024d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e7529f6-6980-56a6-af05-5ca35dd024d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259205} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9c70dbb-3a1d-59ff-9170-ea4bcf75b936" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259204}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "afea9ca1-a406-5faa-9e2a-dda897028d2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-278308" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}-6x+8>0?$\n\n$x\\in (-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cup ($\n{{field-2}}\n$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x+8 & >0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-4) & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x-2)(x-4)=0.$ The roots are $x=2$ and $x=4,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,2),$ $(2,4),$ and $(4,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $(2,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x-2)(x-4)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x-2)(x-4)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x-2)(x-4)$ is positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,2)$ and $(4,\\infty ).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}-6x+8>0$ for all \n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (4,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a186b166-928c-5bad-a996-3bdf6af419f5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "629dbfeb-8dc3-5f39-8e05-f05c351569fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "629dbfeb-8dc3-5f39-8e05-f05c351569fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259229} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d636d3b-e96b-5e1c-b78b-028540cfddae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259228} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8261317c-0bee-507f-b6dc-f94b7ba3293e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8261317c-0bee-507f-b6dc-f94b7ba3293e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259231} :answer-field/id #uuid "b33f94b9-983c-5726-8b8f-69adf3effaa7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259230} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73d4794d-e6b6-50d2-9a4c-312c4a547b2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73d4794d-e6b6-50d2-9a4c-312c4a547b2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259233} :answer-field/id #uuid "be4de963-e6bd-5c21-be4e-be63b64e47c1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259232}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "baa13e69-8ea6-5662-aba0-a6423851ad65" :question/math-academy-id "q-278408" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}-7x+10\\ge 0?$\n\n$x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$]\\cup [$\n{{field-3}}\n$,\\,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-7x+10 & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-5) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x-2)(x-5)=0.$ The roots are $x=2$ and $x=5,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,2),$ $(2,5),$ and $(5,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $(2,5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x-2)(x-5)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x-2)(x-5)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x-2)(x-5)$ is zero or positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,2)$ and $(5,\\infty ).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=2$ and $x=5.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}-7x+10\\ge 0$ for all \n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,2]\\cup [5,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259234 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b23c4da-7a8d-5076-a4e9-8bcde40bc088" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Table of Signs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e515fca7-d384-5d66-a1d6-049e32ecf2f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259236} {:answer/id #uuid "8af5ce15-e8cf-5aa3-bd2b-5ff924815fa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8af5ce15-e8cf-5aa3-bd2b-5ff924815fa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259237} :answer-field/id #uuid "190f715e-8178-501c-a5fe-f56a5c43a5be" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259235} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50ce3c0f-09e2-5ee9-9d78-56fb4988d116" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259239} {:answer/id #uuid "46e00440-7bea-5ee9-91b6-1b33585d2d22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46e00440-7bea-5ee9-91b6-1b33585d2d22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259240} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb50c58a-e653-5d5d-a2be-7821a4a7e44c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259238} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a2ea4cf-4e02-5d81-9a2c-e35e147e2d11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259242} {:answer/id #uuid "edf49be4-466c-5d14-8b4a-d8dd032a788a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a2ea4cf-4e02-5d81-9a2c-e35e147e2d11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259242} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee27fcfc-c49a-5f2d-ac9b-76d82cff94ff" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259241}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed24486a-03ba-53f0-a903-d0d663446f22" :question/math-academy-id "q-278465" :question/problem "Choose the appropriate signs for the table shown below.\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,15)$ | $(15,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-15$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "- On the interval $(-\\infty ,0),$ we can choose a test point ${x}_{1}=-1.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-15 & =-1-15=(-),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we fill in the table as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,15)$ | $(15,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-15$ | $-$ |  |  |\n\n- On the interval $(0,15),$ we can choose a test point ${x}_{1}=10.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-15 & =10-15=(-),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we fill in the table as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,15)$ | $(15,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-15$ | $-$ | $-$ |  |\n\n- On the interval $(15,\\infty ),$ we can choose a test point ${x}_{1}=100.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-15 & =100-15=(+),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we fill in the table as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,15)$ | $(15,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-15$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b23c4da-7a8d-5076-a4e9-8bcde40bc088" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Table of Signs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b62c04b0-6812-530d-9197-4668529b28af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259246} {:answer/id #uuid "120d53c6-a3cc-5667-9eff-f384ec59f0d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "120d53c6-a3cc-5667-9eff-f384ec59f0d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259247} :answer-field/id #uuid "db822a7c-c5af-5c9e-9c80-a0498c62bbec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259245} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c5a20c9-ecca-5e41-a63a-f46edb4f3e0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259249} {:answer/id #uuid "81895b6d-ca1e-5d8a-883e-481679c1d5f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81895b6d-ca1e-5d8a-883e-481679c1d5f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259250} :answer-field/id #uuid "531c01f1-1bd3-5f91-bd15-26bac604208a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259248} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36cdcc63-54f6-5ea7-9963-7b4a2ef25625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259252} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2bf3e3-5f95-5505-858e-1164eaee5d70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36cdcc63-54f6-5ea7-9963-7b4a2ef25625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259252} :answer-field/id #uuid "75a0e999-d0c5-5624-a4cf-f4582a83b873" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259251}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "03004bfd-df6a-59c0-9192-a6eab9a85f53" :question/math-academy-id "q-278488" :question/problem "What are the missing entries in the sign table below?\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-12)$ | $(-12,11)$ | $(11,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-11$ | {{field-1}} | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+12$ | {{field-2}} | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $(x-11)(x+12)$ | {{field-3}} | $-$ | $+$ |" :question/worked-solution "On the interval $(-\\infty ,-12),$ we can choose a test point ${x}_{1}=-20.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-11 & =-20-11=(-), \\\\ x+12 & =-20+12=(-), \\\\ (x-11)(x+12) & =(-)\\times (-)=(+),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we fill in the table as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-12)$ | $(-12,11)$ | $(11,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+12$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $(x-11)(x+12)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53b26ee4-53af-5660-a9e6-8e5cca8b3e1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cf57b73-5ad2-5eab-bf6d-fb4d095f013d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cf57b73-5ad2-5eab-bf6d-fb4d095f013d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259256} :answer-field/id #uuid "06303f5e-9a77-5a76-93dc-2cd6a3f05bfe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259255}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0df3445d-c4fe-56f7-8f72-bac44d3d2fd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-278536" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx-2=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259257 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53b26ee4-53af-5660-a9e6-8e5cca8b3e1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3b58eef-9b07-5e62-8dfd-06cdd9792dd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3b58eef-9b07-5e62-8dfd-06cdd9792dd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259259} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3d0c0c1-1bcd-51f6-8e4a-70dcf5913bcc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259258}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8e85e93c-3cfa-5608-97e9-59cee6be71eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-278543" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-6}{{x}^{2}+x-2}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-6}{{x}^{2}+x-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)(x+2)}{(x-1)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)\\cancel{(x+2)}}{(x-1)\\cancel{(x+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-3}{x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx-1=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6bca57a-bbd7-5135-b4d1-5394e2880a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6bca57a-bbd7-5135-b4d1-5394e2880a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259262} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bbf306f-0153-5b3f-845e-2e452538f0cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259261}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f2dc1ae-4892-50b9-981f-808f974086b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-278551" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{4}{2{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-1}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4}{2{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2{x}^{2}(x-1)+(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)(x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n(2{x}^{2}+1)(x-1)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x-1=0$ gives the solution $x=1.$\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}+1=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptote is $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259603 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36d4bf37-4818-58ac-9e39-75ce8ceef06a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a \"Less Than or Equal To\" Inequality Given the Graph of the Corresponding Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fe08400-ef5a-5e19-86dd-e40b5f494f94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fe08400-ef5a-5e19-86dd-e40b5f494f94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259605} :answer-field/id #uuid "c50dbe77-d135-57d0-9cb9-7fb3abe07433" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259604} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5ddc88e-e53a-5e20-bf3a-ef5c14a3bacd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5ddc88e-e53a-5e20-bf3a-ef5c14a3bacd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259607} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dba7302-3b20-5a28-be96-eec1f27df504" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259606} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fdc06ea-d729-5a48-a5df-ecd6a0c913ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fdc06ea-d729-5a48-a5df-ecd6a0c913ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259609} :answer-field/id #uuid "29b8d5e2-d38b-571f-847a-19e80ad52aee" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259608}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a1abfe16-544e-536a-be8b-9d5556c70b80" :question/math-academy-id "q-326144" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/9e2575f2e20fb3146a92768eeedb32bed1bdb1ddb69f68b55c925a8f9898b695.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $-(x-5)(x+3)\\le 0.$\n\nThe solution to the inequality is $x\\le $\n{{field-1}}\nor $x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n-(x-5)(x+3)\\le 0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*less than or equal to*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-(x-5)(x+3)\n$$\n\nis *below* the $x$-axis or *touches* this axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/68d77d138842f95fbc055d5f8a0adda2bb7b04f8a133ce02d4813a54265e883b.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x\\le -3$ or $x\\ge 5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259629 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc419917-fe34-5bf9-8bf0-1b022fce11ff" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a \"Less Than\" Inequality Given the Graph of the Corresponding Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0e18a3e-e437-5238-81ec-648bc6251417" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0e18a3e-e437-5238-81ec-648bc6251417" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259631} :answer-field/id #uuid "8337e4b4-8dc2-5dac-b1a4-957add5cd55d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259630} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c14e51f8-54c9-5150-b5e6-e068eb1aef2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c14e51f8-54c9-5150-b5e6-e068eb1aef2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259633} :answer-field/id #uuid "64529d7f-ca5b-5e31-aee6-dc1acb257443" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259632} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32bdd285-de23-5070-ba8c-7fdd6f29175b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32bdd285-de23-5070-ba8c-7fdd6f29175b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259635} :answer-field/id #uuid "287ff432-c06b-535e-b2f8-726a3d52b5e7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259634}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d4e62bc6-3f98-510b-80a4-0b7f3a3e5668" :question/math-academy-id "q-327473" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/0279244ec15b12209fa0a18b46bb9a52622878221c9d424b082f73feefae109d.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $-(x-4)(x+1)<0.$\n\nThe solution to the inequality is \n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nor $x>$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n-(x-4)(x+1)<0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*less than*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola\n\n$$\ny=-(x-4)(x+1)\n$$\n\nis *below* the $x$-axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/3153ee62691b0000cbf249553c93a7304c3a2908d2ea7810874c5ed37e68a412.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x<-1$ or $x>4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259636 :