Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/55b40acad6aed639934c751bbc0b5dadcf7a6a1169931d19e9bec9de60401f81.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $-{x}^{2}+4x+5>0.$\n\nThe solution to the inequality is\n{{field-1}}\n$<x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}+4x+5>0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*greater than*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola\n\n$$\ny=-{x}^{2}+4x+5\n$$\n\n \nis *above* the $x$-axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/373430993d1b1d46a92b44ed897c5e4570b6a9c9072e5b9a4af2b854b7801c81.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $-1<x<5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "283415d8-47d7-5d42-8836-0c0b0c4efc85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Values Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c40a2ff-aaab-5f5f-84a6-6f23e65956a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c40a2ff-aaab-5f5f-84a6-6f23e65956a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259721} :answer-field/id #uuid "df6b3482-1bd2-53a3-bb18-5c91166f75ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259720}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ba27561-086b-5243-b9dc-bb922689b561" :question/math-academy-id "q-332487" :question/problem "Using the fact that $\\frac{11\\pi }{12}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{6},$\ncalculate the **exact** value of $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{11\\pi }{12}).$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{11\\pi }{12})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(x+y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{11\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "283415d8-47d7-5d42-8836-0c0b0c4efc85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Values Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e10e5597-fec0-529e-b36f-156c634cadd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e10e5597-fec0-529e-b36f-156c634cadd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259724} :answer-field/id #uuid "6081c00e-fe42-5588-96f8-ae69b2f862f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259723}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4aec515d-e991-53f9-902d-f194d5baa993" :question/math-academy-id "q-332488" :question/problem "Using the fact that $\\frac{17\\pi }{12}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$\ncalculate the **exact** value of $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{17\\pi }{12}).$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{17\\pi }{12})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(x+y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $y=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{17\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{6}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "283415d8-47d7-5d42-8836-0c0b0c4efc85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Values Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "584f48f4-d27c-5487-ba3d-f4d6f8bd5cb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "584f48f4-d27c-5487-ba3d-f4d6f8bd5cb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259727} :answer-field/id #uuid "efc71595-d10d-5843-97da-a8f4dd720b97" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259726}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef62c9f9-a095-50cb-9ce1-245f17a582f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-332492" :question/problem "Using the fact that ${75}^{∘}={45}^{∘}+{30}^{∘},$\ncalculate the **exact** value of $\\operatorname{cos}({75}^{∘}).$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}({75}^{∘})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(x+y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $x={45}^{∘}$ and $y={30}^{∘}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}({75}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}({45}^{∘}+{30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}({45}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({30}^{∘})-\\operatorname{sin}({45}^{∘})\\operatorname{sin}({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "283415d8-47d7-5d42-8836-0c0b0c4efc85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Values Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fabff5cf-2fca-5042-ba54-3685ecd36b7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fabff5cf-2fca-5042-ba54-3685ecd36b7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259730} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fbc4e67-494b-534c-b603-48fdc7e2bbb1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259729}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43a1b2b0-4d34-5f4d-8a4e-7b0cc94830d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-332497" :question/problem "Using the fact that $-{165}^{∘}={60}^{∘}-{225}^{∘},$\ncalculate the **exact** value of $\\operatorname{cos}(-{165}^{∘}).$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}(-{165}^{∘})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(x-y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $x={60}^{∘}$ and $y={225}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(-{165}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}({60}^{∘}-{225}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}({60}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({225}^{∘})+\\operatorname{sin}({60}^{∘})\\operatorname{sin}({225}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259731 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "283415d8-47d7-5d42-8836-0c0b0c4efc85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Values Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51848a55-9dcd-5809-8d1d-b784e728b2d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259733}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51848a55-9dcd-5809-8d1d-b784e728b2d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259733} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d6cd35c-99df-53bf-a223-b226bd97ad44" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259732}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c17df135-ee84-5f51-838d-783803b3c840" :question/math-academy-id "q-332500" :question/problem "Using the fact that ${120}^{∘}={150}^{∘}-{30}^{∘},$\ncalculate the **exact** value of $\\operatorname{cos}({120}^{∘}).$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}({120}^{∘})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(x-y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $x={150}^{∘}$ and $y={30}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}({120}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}({150}^{∘}-{30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}({150}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({30}^{∘})+\\operatorname{sin}({150}^{∘})\\operatorname{sin}({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259734 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6a58962-cb06-519a-8c64-0dfa3b9c14d7" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Degrees Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d636191-15fc-5c2a-b9c9-8f4943ad9652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d636191-15fc-5c2a-b9c9-8f4943ad9652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259736} :answer-field/id #uuid "063dfd94-2bbe-514c-a093-9dceaa47e537" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259735} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ba279fd-6d34-5a0a-a655-9a8a46435c31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ba279fd-6d34-5a0a-a655-9a8a46435c31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259738} :answer-field/id #uuid "7aa0ccc9-583d-58a8-865d-696a168eb38e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259737}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b25c8bbb-7711-5aeb-aaeb-86aea98f9435" :question/math-academy-id "q-332509" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{cos}(x+{330}^{∘})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}(x+{330}^{∘})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}x$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{cos}v-\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v={330}^{∘}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(x+{330}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}{330}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}{330}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9bfd9e9-603e-50ae-8cd1-be79b88aa087" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Polynomial With Leading Coefficients as a Difference of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78d54ce1-1926-52f7-a125-746492624d69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9 + 4x^{2})(3 + 2x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271964} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc30656-f51d-5c0c-85c4-8b73bb40d176" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3 − 2x)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271965} {:answer/id #uuid "b7ae355b-6b42-566b-8c3f-f11ecc3b3338" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9 + 4x^{2})(3 + 2x)(3 − 2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271966} {:answer/id #uuid "6e531086-3d80-5501-86ff-7b2ff5cabe08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3 + 2x)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271967} {:answer/id #uuid "017d2aff-57f5-5e7f-a140-a77442717ac9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "017d2aff-57f5-5e7f-a140-a77442717ac9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271968} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a8dbc91-891e-5ae9-8482-1ef35a1e0a41" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274672}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a639602e-39c5-51b8-b4e5-2591b688f7fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-37702" :question/problem "Factor the expression $\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{2}-24.$" :question/worked-solution "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that ${x}^{4}$ has a factor of $\\frac{3}{2}.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $\\frac{3}{2}$ in our expression, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{2}-24=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {x}^{4}-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 16=\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{4}-16)\n$$\n\nThe expression in parentheses can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{4}-16)=\\frac{3}{2}({({x}^{2})}^{2}-{4}^{2})\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}({({x}^{2})}^{2}-{4}^{2})=\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+4)({x}^{2}-4)\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares. Therefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+4)({x}^{2}-4) & =\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+4)({x}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60c99086-75e7-5d91-b425-4e1b6f582bcc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "614c7d2b-c5b8-5c4e-87ee-8be0365d33ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{-\\frac32,1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272004} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5b9ae0-f81b-57ea-8392-ab8620ad66ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\frac32)-2,1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272005} {:answer/id #uuid "a948388b-1c17-5d4d-a076-ecb6a82f2bc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\frac32)+1,1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272006} {:answer/id #uuid "fea33a7c-3701-5061-880a-8dfea034075b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\frac32)-1,1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272007} {:answer/id #uuid "db8c345c-dac4-5fdb-981d-42c518d1dd18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\frac32)+2,1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "614c7d2b-c5b8-5c4e-87ee-8be0365d33ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{-\\frac32,1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272004} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4a0c079-03c9-5210-a3a7-5de11546d676" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274680}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81b756f0-9b92-5a8e-a082-00d2ced4e8ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-39790" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{2{x}^{2}+x-3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x+1}{(2x+3)(x-1)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+3)(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260294 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f94b20f-9190-5851-ba54-e7687a223a41" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Geometric Series Using Sigma Notation With Simplifications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a3dd296-f355-5e5a-9457-659e85cb1672" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{30}(-2){4}^{n}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272039} {:answer/id #uuid "e50d1396-dce1-538e-a23c-428127168e74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{30}(-2){4}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272040} {:answer/id #uuid "6b8bdef2-56da-5bca-a177-a4bbca3eb83c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac12\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{30}(-2){4}^{n}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272041} {:answer/id #uuid "d147106b-15e3-5cc7-84a2-f616ae8bb578" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{30}(-2){4}^{n}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272042} {:answer/id #uuid "2ed696f1-e352-5849-b90f-a42cb3b465e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{30}(-2){4}^{n}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e50d1396-dce1-538e-a23c-428127168e74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{30}(-2){4}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272040} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e45b8b7-26cf-57a0-a4e5-2831a6f6e608" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274687}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fcaed1ea-a740-59c1-9f0c-86cba46523b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-40003" :question/problem "The first term of a geometric sequence is $-8$ and the common ratio is $4.$ Written in sigma notation, what is the sum of the first $30$ terms?" :question/worked-solution "First, we must express the series in the form:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ denotes the first term of the series, $r$ denotes the common ratio, and $N$ denotes the number of terms in the series. In this case, ${a}_{1}=-8,$ $r=4$ and $N=30.$\n\nExpressing the geometric series in sigma notation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{S}_{30}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{30}(-8){4}^{n-1}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nFinally, we can simplify the above using the laws of exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{30}(-8){4}^{n-1} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{30}(-2)\\cdot 4\\cdot {4}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{30}(-2)\\cdot {4}^{1+n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{30}(-2){4}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "283415d8-47d7-5d42-8836-0c0b0c4efc85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Values Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ca1348f-bb75-5092-9714-2b7daf7fa308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272122} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b8261c-674e-5f2c-965c-2970f6e23c25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272123} {:answer/id #uuid "6e40dfd2-2775-5a56-a824-5250c0894ab3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272124} {:answer/id #uuid "26a21fc6-b740-5359-b850-5527f6d26825" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272125} {:answer/id #uuid "179303dc-1ced-5f9e-bea1-465f3592a182" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ca1348f-bb75-5092-9714-2b7daf7fa308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272122} :answer-field/id #uuid "5da0d2c8-ecd8-5179-b3a2-b0b95b7d5bb3" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274704}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c73da58-2595-51a8-a8de-bdfae4bbae89" :question/math-academy-id "q-43381" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\operatorname{cos}{165}^{∘}.$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${165}^{∘}={120}^{∘}+{45}^{∘}.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(x+y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $x={120}^{∘}$ and $y={45}^{∘}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}({120}^{∘}+{45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}{120}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}{45}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}{120}^{∘}\\operatorname{sin}{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "831f9aa0-099e-5e0c-8a9d-c4828b629f64" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "727b3dbe-243d-51d7-ad89-6f0722599373" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272127} {:answer/id #uuid "30b8bbc6-8d79-57b6-963a-a29d7bfbf3af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− \\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272128} {:answer/id #uuid "1a8d07e1-b6e7-533d-8223-aa19e4f5eb88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x-\\operatorname{cos}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272129} {:answer/id #uuid "44a5f8bb-222f-5333-9048-efc9761f4c12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272130} {:answer/id #uuid "de54f6de-4e88-5e0f-a171-120f1e7c91a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− \\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a8d07e1-b6e7-533d-8223-aa19e4f5eb88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x-\\operatorname{cos}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272129} :answer-field/id #uuid "72014007-a5a2-5a44-bd92-c0ade093a0d1" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274705}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3e55af46-7aa0-5eeb-8a65-43726ccf1418" :question/math-academy-id "q-43384" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}(x-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{cos}v+\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(x-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x-\\operatorname{cos}x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "143766e0-0ef5-5caf-b699-d394d4a707e9" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Products"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4f8d2ae-2680-5b6a-8595-4541e477982c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(3\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{5}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272132} {:answer/id #uuid "f8be012f-fc00-56a3-9973-f6ce8d8c4f28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(3\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{5}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272133} {:answer/id #uuid "c137da58-45da-546f-98eb-9fc3c86b62db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(3\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{5}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272134} {:answer/id #uuid "ccfca8a6-bd65-5051-a9b3-ffdde541c086" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(3\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{5}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272135} {:answer/id #uuid "d482d18e-7623-5c59-8b65-9fb02774d5fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d482d18e-7623-5c59-8b65-9fb02774d5fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272136} :answer-field/id #uuid "eeee0ae8-2b13-5a67-b4bc-66de714b223a" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274706}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b13a054-dc23-5be0-bdef-7ff3c961a76d" :question/math-academy-id "q-43462" :question/problem "Express $6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{10})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{10})$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{10}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{10})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}) & =\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{10})=\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{10})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{10})=3\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{10})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{10})=3\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{5}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d191b0f-0582-5af5-bd4b-2b090cb7be05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{-\\frac12,0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272211} {:answer/id #uuid "88f82ffe-ab63-5efb-b11c-a3bd49c35b22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\frac12)-2,0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272212} {:answer/id #uuid "c040926f-5cf1-56a7-b915-7026a85e4f93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\frac12)-1,0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272213} {:answer/id #uuid "1d55b88d-6700-5543-9ef5-a1d49d197296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\frac12)+2,0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272214} {:answer/id #uuid "c85d2042-c060-50ff-8e07-d409c719f7a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\frac12)+1,0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d191b0f-0582-5af5-bd4b-2b090cb7be05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{-\\frac12,0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272211} :answer-field/id #uuid "4da4a2e7-942b-5717-9e3c-34000a26ef8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274722}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "119a8c59-2e06-5773-a076-fad28dc30f8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-4698" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{8{x}^{4}+x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{8{x}^{4}+x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{x(8{x}^{3}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{x[(2x)^{3}+{1}^{3}]} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{x(2x+1)(4{x}^{2}-2x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the sum of cubes formula to factor the denominator.\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx(2x+1)(4{x}^{2}-2x+1)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x=0$ gives the solution $x=0.$\n\n- Solving $2x+1=0$ gives the solution $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The equation $4{x}^{2}-2x+1=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-2)^{2}-4(4)(1) \\\\  & =4-16 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=0$ and $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260560 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7df31c9-b27a-5ce2-93c8-178ba96da000" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "987af911-4ebf-55ff-9299-da1bf609f416" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(640)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272335} {:answer/id #uuid "4df5bd3f-c131-5bc3-b557-81f25dade442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(640)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272336} {:answer/id #uuid "57fd51ea-ffb6-5d1d-8163-9a50252cd6fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(640)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272337} {:answer/id #uuid "dce13ff5-83a5-504b-8a10-12e5af235d1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(640)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272338} {:answer/id #uuid "cc3e1cec-22d4-5808-9f90-f47011c1a422" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "640" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272339}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc3e1cec-22d4-5808-9f90-f47011c1a422" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "640" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272339} :answer-field/id #uuid "f58c59b9-f809-5d2a-9528-b36c07562bdb" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274747}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9cc8a79a-7802-592e-80ef-d176d2e37ddd" :question/math-academy-id "q-48608" :question/problem "The birth rate of a krill population is modeled by $B(t)=5\\cdot {2}^{0.7t}$ thousands of tons per year, where $t$ is the time, in years, since $1998.$ What was the birth rate, in thousands of tons per year, for the krill in $2008?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $B(t)$ models the birth rate for the krill, in thousands of tons, $t$ years since $1998.$ Since we want the birth rate in $2008$, then $t=10$ years.\n\nThus, the birth rate for the krill in $2008$ was\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(10) & =5\\cdot {2}^{0.7(10)} \\\\  & =5\\cdot {2}^{7} \\\\  & =640.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the birth rate for the krill in $2008$ was $640$ thousand tons per year."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5337e4-9289-52fc-ba01-a0e7a8df6285" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Polynomial as a Difference of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75f9bdcf-4fd2-5a93-b1bf-bd4942f955ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(({t}^{2}+4)(t+2)(t-2))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272485} {:answer/id #uuid "ac61ca37-0311-54ee-ab13-876d85c14425" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(({t}^{2}+4)(t+2)(t-2))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272486} {:answer/id #uuid "31e0f743-6cff-5302-9eca-a85f27c1cc3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(({t}^{2}+4)(t+2)(t-2))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272487} {:answer/id #uuid "71831a01-4298-5e00-a2ca-128c959f7ab5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({t}^{2}+4)(t+2)(t-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272488} {:answer/id #uuid "c5cd6b2f-42ce-56b8-a79b-3a7514ac8b65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(({t}^{2}+4)(t+2)(t-2))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71831a01-4298-5e00-a2ca-128c959f7ab5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({t}^{2}+4)(t+2)(t-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272488} :answer-field/id #uuid "c99c5ae5-8223-5f24-8336-1e113b886a5d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274777}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "706f1b39-71a2-517a-b05a-ddde27e028de" :question/math-academy-id "q-5156" :question/problem "Factor ${t}^{4}-16.$" :question/worked-solution "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n{t}^{4}-16={({t}^{2})}^{2}-{4}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{({t}^{2})}^{2}-{4}^{2}=({t}^{2}+4)({t}^{2}-4)\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares. Therefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({t}^{2}+4)({t}^{2}-4) & =({t}^{2}+4)({t}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =({t}^{2}+4)(t+2)(t-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9bfd9e9-603e-50ae-8cd1-be79b88aa087" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Polynomial With Leading Coefficients as a Difference of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19c76c53-d58b-5e01-9975-7d750fc9fe3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9 + 4x^{2})(3 + 2x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272495} {:answer/id #uuid "ca68f24f-7b9a-55e3-944e-01253de8f923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272496} {:answer/id #uuid "6d2a728d-fed4-5f87-9499-fcc63431d517" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3 − 2x)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272497} {:answer/id #uuid "c43d3b39-8246-5800-a49b-f607edd6c7c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9 + 4x^{2})(3 + 2x)(3 − 2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272498} {:answer/id #uuid "b2c2cfd7-0300-510b-9caf-b5e5d083d045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3 + 2x)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca68f24f-7b9a-55e3-944e-01253de8f923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272496} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fbf0971-6667-5d97-89d0-8f26127e99fd" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274779}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a9dd0e6-6456-5413-b50a-4bf96b104572" :question/math-academy-id "q-5165" :question/problem "Factor $3{x}^{4}-48.$" :question/worked-solution "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that all of the terms in our expression have a common factor of $3.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}-48=3\\cdot {x}^{4}-3\\cdot 16=3({x}^{4}-16)\n$$\n\nThe expression in parentheses can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n3({x}^{4}-16)=3({({x}^{2})}^{2}-{4}^{2})\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n3({({x}^{2})}^{2}-{4}^{2})=3({x}^{2}+4)({x}^{2}-4)\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares. Therefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({x}^{2}+4)({x}^{2}-4) & =3({x}^{2}+4)({x}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260712 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91bf0b47-327f-5b82-a87a-307b7eda9e1c" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Arithmetic Series Using Sigma Notation when Not All Terms are Shown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7d6aaae-d632-54af-a812-fc2dddbeb086" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac12\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(7-3n)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272505} {:answer/id #uuid "165cec17-541d-5afa-8e96-edf44485de61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(7-3n)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272506} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7c4e86-f557-551a-a0a8-7a2b44857fea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(7-3n)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272507} {:answer/id #uuid "49a1cddd-21ad-579d-9c21-6142aef99b36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(7-3n)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272508} {:answer/id #uuid "703cc3ed-9f6a-51f5-90dd-c52b3b421307" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(7-3n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "703cc3ed-9f6a-51f5-90dd-c52b3b421307" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(7-3n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272509} :answer-field/id #uuid "32ada5f9-e69b-56a5-92f8-332b7131ec60" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274781}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b147fea-e6ff-5e9f-b32f-ff0ababdac80" :question/math-academy-id "q-5236" :question/problem "Express the following arithmetic series using sigma notation.\n\n$$\n4+1-2-…-20-23\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term ${a}_{1}=4$ and the common difference is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =1-(4) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =4+(n-1)(-3) \\\\  & =4-3n+3 \\\\  & =7-3n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe don't know the index of the last term, but we can find it using the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-23 \\\\ 7-3n & =-23 \\\\ 7+23 & =3n \\\\ 30 & =3n \\\\ 10 & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are $10$ terms in total, from $n=1$ to $n=10.$ So, the sigma notation for the given sum is:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(7-3n)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6813b06-c450-599f-9b64-0588ec4142d9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum of an Arithmetic Series Given in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94be40a5-510e-5f9d-9e47-b1f578f446a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "224" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272510} {:answer/id #uuid "134d9be0-3850-5807-a1e4-a8cb098fbb6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "432" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272511} {:answer/id #uuid "0db1b68b-b2af-579f-9218-99b5601fb9d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272512} {:answer/id #uuid "25f1fa82-c499-528a-a579-1fd0a67fd3de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "816" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272513} {:answer/id #uuid "0a923823-0e38-5c01-8e2b-72f852420cfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "363" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0db1b68b-b2af-579f-9218-99b5601fb9d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272512} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffee1101-8464-5a3e-884e-b3d6c6635755" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274782}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac22c321-341c-5b1a-8d5d-082d85416290" :question/math-academy-id "q-5300" :question/problem "What is the sum of the arithmetic series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{8}(-2n)?$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{N}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, ${a}_{N}$ is the last term, and $N$ is the total number of terms.\n\nGiven the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{8}(-2n)$ in sigma notation, we note that\n\n- the total number of terms is $N=8$\n\n- the first term is ${a}_{1}=-2\\cdot 1=-2$\n\n- the last term is ${a}_{8}=-2\\cdot 8=-16$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{8} & =\\frac{8}{2}(-2+(-16)) \\\\  & =4(-18) \\\\  & =-72\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb53971d-15be-579e-86f5-4fc8d0c10d6d" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Arithmetic Series Using Sigma Notation when All Terms are Shown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5637dab2-59c9-57c8-b700-6f8c93cef9dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}(6n-2)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272614} {:answer/id #uuid "a7f531e7-e9ab-5790-878b-591079be33a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}(6n-2)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272615} {:answer/id #uuid "445232b5-ff20-582c-ac83-2700804faa4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}(6n-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272616} {:answer/id #uuid "1afb1eeb-33f7-5cf4-b873-03979fc21d14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}(6n-2)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272617} {:answer/id #uuid "e976ca47-cf2a-56ce-953f-1c522cc060ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac12\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}(6n-2)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "445232b5-ff20-582c-ac83-2700804faa4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}(6n-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272616} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f35a45b-96f9-5d67-a1ad-012d25a03f8e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274806}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "def295e5-6bc1-5660-bf4c-3c6ddeefa998" :question/math-academy-id "q-6447" :question/problem "The arithmetic series $4+10+16+22+28+34+40$ can be written in sigma notation as" :question/worked-solution "The first term is ${a}_{1}=4$ and the common difference is: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =10-4 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =4+(n-1)(6) \\\\  & =4+6n-6 \\\\  & =6n-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are $7$ terms in total, from $n=1$ to $n=7.$ So the sigma notation for the given sum is:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}(6n-2)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260848 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6813b06-c450-599f-9b64-0588ec4142d9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum of an Arithmetic Series Given in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80fa0ef1-26ce-570a-85be-6afc4e279cb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "224" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272629} {:answer/id #uuid "b900d297-4d77-55d4-8e0e-23dae15f3ff5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "432" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272630} {:answer/id #uuid "335bb760-f3a1-595f-8bd0-51097c059daf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "816" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272631} {:answer/id #uuid "5b73611d-3dea-5f18-bca6-3a019faaed93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "363" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272632} {:answer/id #uuid "37d60676-d6f8-5966-93b9-3551beb331b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37d60676-d6f8-5966-93b9-3551beb331b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272633} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea08ae68-71f2-5b86-8967-6dce6cca9cc8" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274809}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "51f91c45-43d2-5fcb-8a9f-a87826b50b9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-6491" :question/problem "What is the sum of the arithmetic series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(n+5)?$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{N}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, ${a}_{N}$ is the last term, and $N$ is the total number of terms.\n\nGiven the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(n+5)$ in sigma notation, we note that\n\n- the total number of terms is $N=10$\n\n- the first term is ${a}_{1}=1+5=6$\n\n- the last term is ${a}_{10}=10+5=15$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{10} & =\\frac{10}{2}(6+15) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 21 \\\\  & =105\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "283415d8-47d7-5d42-8836-0c0b0c4efc85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Values Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "476380fb-bf5b-5cbe-9312-7e56c24c7681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272669} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5455c3-bcba-50be-9cd0-9e66372db288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272670} {:answer/id #uuid "25a5c1a9-78be-5979-8358-9c7aa9056985" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272671} {:answer/id #uuid "26e6ff46-8ffb-57b4-8fb4-dd1f9e77fc46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272672} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd7e7c4-cc32-5115-8214-828bcfdad186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "476380fb-bf5b-5cbe-9312-7e56c24c7681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272669} :answer-field/id #uuid "3593cbd3-7680-5c97-91a5-1b53dac5ded6" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274817}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d9857563-42e2-5591-8956-ff251a3df14e" :question/math-academy-id "q-65551" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{13\\pi }{12}).$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $\\frac{13\\pi }{12}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(x-y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{13\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "283415d8-47d7-5d42-8836-0c0b0c4efc85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Values Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d27721d8-5ad2-5e34-8d4a-a89d941daa48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272674} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e28622-61dc-555d-82f6-7beb6b03404e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272675} {:answer/id #uuid "69f87189-bd48-5d74-b844-b7ccf0f502c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272676} {:answer/id #uuid "7d49d358-043d-51e1-b6b5-83d82e05daa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272677} {:answer/id #uuid "175d9b01-580b-5901-ac2d-5962c0ae3eec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d27721d8-5ad2-5e34-8d4a-a89d941daa48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272674} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d01aa82-68ba-59b4-aa2a-0a25b4894096" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274818}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7da7ff7e-6169-5165-9584-01d86caac744" :question/math-academy-id "q-65556" :question/problem "Find the exact value of  $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{12}).$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $\\frac{\\pi }{12}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(x-y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f9683d9-05b9-5a42-9f81-0170a15dfac1" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Ratios"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7378aaf-fe67-5e5a-8548-33cc82f76e8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(2\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272679} {:answer/id #uuid "e0136367-14d4-59a1-a480-e098bb14c693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(2\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272680} {:answer/id #uuid "bf295a9b-1473-51f9-8fa3-515bfb1e185c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(2\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272681} {:answer/id #uuid "985f2dc6-7754-57b0-9643-d70b40837ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(2\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272682} {:answer/id #uuid "b59fbeae-9c2c-56ee-a1cf-22b280887e7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b59fbeae-9c2c-56ee-a1cf-22b280887e7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272683} :answer-field/id #uuid "402768fe-ed5f-5a78-958a-6e5b1f2c495f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274819}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e7013fe-76e9-5d3e-a584-12066788f47b" :question/math-academy-id "q-65608" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{{sin}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}2x=2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})=2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{{sin}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{{sin}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261001 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10095048-0db6-5b4e-889f-303f07449367" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions to Exponential Equations when Rearrangement is Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d6375e5-baf6-5ed8-b9bc-24ca0a16e87e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272838} {:answer/id #uuid "c5e9e0ba-d203-5573-8703-904fca8893f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2)(\\ln (4) − 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272839} {:answer/id #uuid "9e5ce1ca-1cc1-591c-b877-bfd74b35f05a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (1)/(2)(\\ln (4) + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272840} {:answer/id #uuid "dc4393b2-f1ae-568e-a477-70cf29549851" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(4)(\\ln (2) − 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272841} {:answer/id #uuid "0e966017-9914-5bc6-8341-8c2fa3537736" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(4)(1 − \\ln (4))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d6375e5-baf6-5ed8-b9bc-24ca0a16e87e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272838} :answer-field/id #uuid "71f9abeb-b5d4-5263-8d62-71a0126a56b7" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274851}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8d597e1-3a70-5551-8ca3-548ecbb3ddce" :question/math-academy-id "q-66901" :question/problem "Given that ${e}^{2h}-4=7,$ find the value of $h$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation into a form that is more convenient for logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2h}-4 & =7 \\\\ {e}^{2h} & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $h:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2h} & =11 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}({e}^{2h}) & =\\operatorname{ln}(11) \\\\ 2h & =\\operatorname{ln}(11) \\\\ h & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}(11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $h=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}(11)$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nh\\approx 1.199\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10095048-0db6-5b4e-889f-303f07449367" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions to Exponential Equations when Rearrangement is Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8959ef6a-7067-52a9-8e75-c2964e5e62d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272843} {:answer/id #uuid "598581e8-1d67-5f5f-80ae-e9fba6b1a61f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2)(\\ln (4) − 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272844} {:answer/id #uuid "dcbbb6ad-90c7-5b1a-81b2-3a090ae6d8cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (1)/(2)(\\ln (4) + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272845} {:answer/id #uuid "7bfc7a4d-b391-5efa-b788-29abbc85c71f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(4)(\\ln (2) − 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272846} {:answer/id #uuid "e16ba7a8-ed68-5f80-9c26-aeffc19dd0e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(4)(1 − \\ln (4))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8959ef6a-7067-52a9-8e75-c2964e5e62d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272843} :answer-field/id #uuid "d79dbaa4-800f-546a-8549-232249a079cc" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274852}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e5cb6a2-7bc8-5f55-bf93-6f94f39a879f" :question/math-academy-id "q-66904" :question/problem "Given that ${e}^{-7p}+3=8,$ find the value of $p$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation into a form that is more convenient for logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-7p}+3 & =8 \\\\ {e}^{-7p} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-7p} & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}({e}^{-7p}) & =\\operatorname{ln}(5) \\\\ -7p & =\\operatorname{ln}(5) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{ln}(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $p=-\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{ln}(5)$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\np\\approx -0.230\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d8c6c93-9d15-5482-8130-25bb9cabf008" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions to Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5cb2370-de3f-5771-b529-7ae0c23bba4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (10) − 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272848} {:answer/id #uuid "fab91503-6942-5be6-b2bd-3991eca7eeb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}7-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272849} {:answer/id #uuid "463a670f-269e-5576-b4d5-8c53df46311b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2 − \\ln (10))/(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272850} {:answer/id #uuid "1ff78ed0-35b4-500b-877a-e023432df869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 − \\ln (10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272851} {:answer/id #uuid "c82f638a-83dc-5b23-8145-ff401d6e4b83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 − 3\\ln (10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fab91503-6942-5be6-b2bd-3991eca7eeb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}7-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272849} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6f2aa9d-0659-5c36-8db8-c1b0211760d9" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274853}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a4fcecca-42f0-5b23-b6e5-a78929f1c209" :question/math-academy-id "q-66963" :question/problem "What is the value of $x$ if ${e}^{2x+3}=\\sqrt[5]{7}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x+3} & =\\sqrt[5]{7} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}({e}^{2x+3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}(\\sqrt[5]{7})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\n2x+3=\\operatorname{ln}(\\sqrt[5]{7})\n$$\n\nNext, we write $\\sqrt[5]{7}$ as ${7}^{1/5}.$ So, we get:\n\n$$\n2x+3=\\operatorname{ln}({7}^{1/5})\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the right-hand side using power law of logarithms and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =\\operatorname{ln}({7}^{1/5}) \\\\ 2x+3 & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}(7) \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}(7)-3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}(7)-3) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}7-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}7-\\frac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "829ac8f1-7ade-5dd9-9f2f-78234a57a257" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum of an Arithmetic Series Given the First and Last Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94be465b-f2e6-5ea2-8cba-ef268600d8df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "138" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272853} {:answer/id #uuid "77f804ff-5287-5f6d-b83e-69ba8ae15da4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "142" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272854} {:answer/id #uuid "ba9de7fb-2045-5c97-9d89-d304b0e233b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-273" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272855} {:answer/id #uuid "c111c419-f7f9-52a0-a021-b5fa0c892cbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "156" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272856} {:answer/id #uuid "b920ea64-8daa-5687-b412-0fbf1c2b065d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba9de7fb-2045-5c97-9d89-d304b0e233b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-273" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272855} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6f46a60-2169-50bf-95e1-63141bbccfd7" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274854}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b0d02cf-7e0a-5619-ba74-9c01c7f880f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-67043" :question/problem "The following arithmetic series has $13$ terms in total. Find the sum of the series.\n\n$$\n-9+(-11)+(-13)+(-15)+⋯+(-33)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{N}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, ${a}_{N}$ is the last term, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nFor our series, we have ${a}_{1}=-9,{a}_{N}=-33$ and $N=13.$ Substituting these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{13} & =\\frac{13}{2}(-9+(-33)) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}(-42) \\\\  & =13(-21) \\\\  & =-273\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "829ac8f1-7ade-5dd9-9f2f-78234a57a257" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum of an Arithmetic Series Given the First and Last Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "885d7f47-19c9-5634-8c76-0ce6d9b91885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "330" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272858} {:answer/id #uuid "58b746a5-aa19-560c-8404-52e75daf4663" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "138" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272859} {:answer/id #uuid "05c6eb9f-4a09-5331-b938-4e2cca7ac650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "142" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272860} {:answer/id #uuid "fc0a966a-3f40-5770-ad1f-3a5cfcb19949" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "156" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272861} {:answer/id #uuid "5dec6b6b-9d70-558d-93ef-9e6cb829823c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "885d7f47-19c9-5634-8c76-0ce6d9b91885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "330" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272858} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f0e58a8-f32b-5cac-bbb9-99842330381a" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274855}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4fced0be-707f-5fb0-9e9e-ae88a44c183d" :question/math-academy-id "q-67047" :question/problem "The following arithmetic series has $15$ terms in total. Find the sum of the series.\n\n$$\n-6+(-2)+2+6+⋯+50\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{N}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, ${a}_{N}$ is the last term, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nFor our series, we have ${a}_{1}=-6,{a}_{N}=50$ and $N=15.$ Substituting these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{15} & =\\frac{15}{2}(-6+50) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot 44 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 22 \\\\  & =330\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261025 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6133cdf6-6931-5363-918f-5ca8cd97b732" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum of an Arithmetic Series Given Two Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ed9c1a5-716f-529e-baa5-ec66483af583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "325" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272873} {:answer/id #uuid "79bc0734-b5f8-5400-8336-e1ad6511bda9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "260" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272874} {:answer/id #uuid "8d7d6d3f-7d8c-5671-944c-71d048afe325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "315" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272875} {:answer/id #uuid "6177d76f-c4ae-59fe-9200-0167cdeafe15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272876} {:answer/id #uuid "e1a66d33-48f4-5014-adaa-d562cface97d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "365" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6177d76f-c4ae-59fe-9200-0167cdeafe15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272876} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b6a9bc3-152f-5039-9234-913c8c1e6f55" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274858}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a41ce80-b8e8-5930-a1cd-1406aa9952fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-67177" :question/problem "An arithmetic series has ${a}_{1}=1$ and ${a}_{5}=3,$ where ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{5}$ are the first and fifth terms of the series, respectively. What is the sum of the first $13$ terms of the series?" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nWe know that ${a}_{5}=3.$ So, using the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\ {a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}+(5-1)d \\\\ 3 & =1+4d \\\\ 4d & =2 \\\\ d & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting ${a}_{1}=1$, $d=\\frac{1}{2}$, and $N=13$ into the above formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{13} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}(2\\cdot 1+(13-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}\\cdot (2+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =13\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =52\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6133cdf6-6931-5363-918f-5ca8cd97b732" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum of an Arithmetic Series Given Two Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e61dfebb-6cad-5568-a5aa-54560038afe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "325" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272878} {:answer/id #uuid "9b9f6691-a7d5-5826-8bd1-9bab17b27c87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "260" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272879} {:answer/id #uuid "7bdce238-754c-5700-b3d7-837d2076612b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-405" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272880} {:answer/id #uuid "506344dc-4c06-50be-ba57-8c4781da790a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "315" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272881} {:answer/id #uuid "9ec150fe-4ce6-526f-bc25-83a02b920d85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "365" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bdce238-754c-5700-b3d7-837d2076612b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-405" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272880} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b5fb156-a5e6-53a7-99c6-f2a68ccff486" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274859}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32b30204-fdd2-5361-b71f-01c0c6f85c10" :question/math-academy-id "q-67183" :question/problem "An arithmetic series has ${a}_{4}=-11$ and ${a}_{11}=-39,$ where ${a}_{4}$ and ${a}_{11}$ are the fourth and eleventh terms of the series, respectively. \nFind the sum of the first $15$ terms of the series." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nSince we know two terms of the arithmetic series, we can find its common difference, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{11}-{a}_{4}}{11-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-39-(-11)}{11-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-28}{7} \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, as ${a}_{4}=-11$, using the formula for the $n$th term, we can compute ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}+(4-1)\\cdot (-4) \\\\ -11 & ={a}_{1}+(-12) \\\\ {a}_{1} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${a}_{1}=1$, $d=-4$, and $N=15$ into the above formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{15} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}(2\\cdot 1+(15-1)\\cdot (-4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot (2-56) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot (-54) \\\\  & =15\\cdot (-27) \\\\  & =-405\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261046 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e551c82-6984-5246-9e74-73fa3b55ffe1" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Arithmetic Series Using Sigma Notation Given Two Terms of the Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5446833-7ffc-5175-8230-ae1765ed0854" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac12\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(-3n+13)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272908} {:answer/id #uuid "4cd0a27b-0bb9-55ed-9b7a-7fa3a36d6039" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(-3n+13)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272909} {:answer/id #uuid "ebdaead2-30d3-53a7-ab0e-af1175eb9d85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(-3n+13)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272910} {:answer/id #uuid "1f018402-7054-5914-b39f-0436ffae0759" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(-3n+13)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272911} {:answer/id #uuid "50564b70-32d2-5572-8379-05264fc247d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(-3n+13)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f018402-7054-5914-b39f-0436ffae0759" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(-3n+13)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272911} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1bed89d-4eed-5b47-98c3-aef650c00404" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274865}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37dec902-223c-5579-835f-ac0779fdd1ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-67281" :question/problem "An arithmetic series has ${a}_{1}=10$ and ${a}_{6}=-5,$ where ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{6}$ are the first and sixth terms of the series, respectively. What is the sum of the first ten terms of the series, expressed using sigma notation?" :question/worked-solution "We know that ${a}_{6}=-5.$ So, using the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\ -5 & =10+(6-1)d \\\\ -5 & =10+5d \\\\ 5d & =-15 \\\\ d & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${a}_{1}=10$ and $d=-3.$ Thus, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =10+(n-1)(-3) \\\\  & =10-3n+3 \\\\  & =-3n+13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, the sum of the first ten terms can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(-3n+13)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6532d0fb-1a24-5177-bd20-2efcb3e13f3d" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Geometric Series With a Negative Common Ratio Using Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7b75ed5-e053-5e95-b00d-790225fd7c77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac12\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-6)^{n}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272918} {:answer/id #uuid "b06716c0-1340-598d-956b-dd32a83db231" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-6)^{n}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272919} {:answer/id #uuid "202eeb07-3e42-5dc1-96d2-465fdeff3146" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-6)^{n}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272920} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a8abb9-18f0-5c37-9052-dbd890ef96ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-6)^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272921} {:answer/id #uuid "761460d2-624e-512a-b92d-fafd4fa25a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-6)^{n}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9a8abb9-18f0-5c37-9052-dbd890ef96ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-6)^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272921} :answer-field/id #uuid "da0f5734-96c4-52ec-be11-8c4b076c03e6" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274867}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a2cca1fe-4d6f-58e1-ae4a-bc0f765dbc4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-67324" :question/problem "The geometric series $-2+12-72+432$ can be expressed using sigma notation as" :question/worked-solution "We must express the given series in the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ denotes the first term of the series, $r$ denotes the common ratio, and $N$ denotes the number of terms in the series. In this case, ${a}_{1}=-2$ and $N=4.$\n\nTo find $r,$ we calculate the ratio of the first two terms in the series:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{12}{-2}=-6\n$$\n\nTherefore, expressing the given geometric series in sigma notation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2+12-72+432 & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}(-2)\\cdot (-6)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can simplify the above using the laws of exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}(-2)\\cdot (-6)^{n-1} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}(-2)\\cdot (-6)^{n}\\cdot (-6)^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}(-2)\\cdot {(-6)}^{-1}\\cdot (-6)^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}(-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{6})(-6)^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-6)^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261058 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f94b20f-9190-5851-ba54-e7687a223a41" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Geometric Series Using Sigma Notation With Simplifications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cbaf769-9c99-56f0-bb3c-39f4a5f8bfd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac12\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(-\\frac{5}{6}){6}^{n}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272923} {:answer/id #uuid "79c0d203-a960-527e-8c9f-af78e06a10c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(-\\frac{5}{6}){6}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272924} {:answer/id #uuid "6e9fc59d-5d30-5257-9703-0ac3e89390da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(-\\frac{5}{6}){6}^{n}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272925} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b77afd-2d80-588b-82ef-b881a747a46b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(-\\frac{5}{6}){6}^{n}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272926} {:answer/id #uuid "b82959f9-24ce-569c-acdc-aa454c90dc1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(-\\frac{5}{6}){6}^{n}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79c0d203-a960-527e-8c9f-af78e06a10c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(-\\frac{5}{6}){6}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272924} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e06f2d4-6419-5287-8b51-e87b65fa213e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274868}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02a78a86-1025-5579-a281-d9673722caa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-67377" :question/problem "The first term of a geometric sequence is $-5$ and the common ratio is $6.$ Written in sigma notation, what is the sum of the first $22$ terms?" :question/worked-solution "First, we must express the series in the form:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ denotes the first term of the series, $r$ denotes the common ratio, and $N$ denotes the number of terms in the series. In this case, ${a}_{1}=-5,$ $r=6$ and $N=22.$\n\nExpressing the geometric series in sigma notation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{S}_{22}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(-5)\\cdot {6}^{n-1}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nFinally, we can simplify the above using the laws of exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(-5)\\cdot {6}^{n-1} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(-5)\\cdot {6}^{n}\\cdot {6}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(-5)\\cdot {6}^{-1}\\cdot {6}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\cdot {6}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(-\\frac{5}{6}){6}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261289 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de7d614-76c9-5133-a4df-95f079467cb9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality With No Constant Term Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6bd3a0b-6609-59b7-90d6-33effe85ecfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273178} {:answer/id #uuid "d4da2191-2669-54ed-9684-6bd66024a168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273179} {:answer/id #uuid "f601488a-6d24-5dcd-a773-f8b8fdf3202f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,\\infty)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273180} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a4f486-093a-56ed-94a4-50f821975ef6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,-1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273181} {:answer/id #uuid "04fb4360-4cd7-568a-ad82-f4820a1a820a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4da2191-2669-54ed-9684-6bd66024a168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273179} :answer-field/id #uuid "653a4553-864e-5e4f-ae3a-620926e358d4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274919}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2f8458e8-0bfe-5745-a2d8-3bb1285a95ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-70921" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}+3x\\ge 0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3x & \\ge 0 \\\\ x(x+3) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $x(x+3)=0.$ The roots are $x=-3$ and $x=0,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-3,0),$ and $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,0)$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+3$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x(x+3)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(-)\\cdot (+)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $x(x+3)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $x(x+3)$ is zero or positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3)$ and $(0,\\infty ).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-3$ and $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}+3x\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [0,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261292 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de7d614-76c9-5133-a4df-95f079467cb9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality With No Constant Term Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "914ffffc-07e2-52fa-8cc5-495a78e2e8f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273183} {:answer/id #uuid "4b5a2ea0-329f-5c95-9965-a9cf54e795f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,\\infty)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273184} {:answer/id #uuid "3954337e-ed32-5e0e-8e99-df9331b44a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,-1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273185} {:answer/id #uuid "b591a2ba-d708-5603-b374-fa5e70095484" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [0,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273186} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f683bf-34ec-59d7-ba4b-19270fc0980e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b591a2ba-d708-5603-b374-fa5e70095484" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [0,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273186} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e4b1991-954d-5e27-8a0a-6ec9731c4d2f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274920}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7ac8026b-ced5-5ad5-9d67-ebf801b0d418" :question/math-academy-id "q-70963" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is $3{x}^{2}-6x\\le 0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x & \\le 0 \\\\ 3x(x-2) & \\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $3x(x-2)=0.$ The roots are $x=0$ and $x=2,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,0),$ $(0,2),$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $3x(x-2)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $3x(x-2)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $3x(x-2)$ is zero or negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(0,2).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $3{x}^{2}-6x\\le 0$ for all $x\\in [0,2].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261368 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fa5bdf9-4b27-5565-9ee1-ecb9f9db4791" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Units of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42e0ec61-b308-5787-a644-41993c32d3cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dollars times points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273293} {:answer/id #uuid "cd301d3f-9325-53c5-898c-049763328947" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Points per dollar" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273294} {:answer/id #uuid "36101866-eb52-591d-818a-d4ebefb82a13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dollars per point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273295} {:answer/id #uuid "72473073-6161-5aef-aef8-d37a8a776e47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dollars" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36101866-eb52-591d-818a-d4ebefb82a13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dollars per point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273295} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2cb933c-9486-555c-8b10-23223f273130" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274942}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dbe3b6c2-cec5-5e7c-9393-5197f7a69152" :question/math-academy-id "q-72091" :question/problem "A student's parents promise to reward her $R(p)$ dollars if she obtains an average of $p$ points in her exams. What are the units of $\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}p}?$" :question/worked-solution "We have that the student's reward $R(p)$ is measured in dollars and the average exam result is measured in points. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(p): & \\text{dollars} \\\\ p: & \\text{points}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}p}$ measures a change in $R(p)$ over a change in $p.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}p}: & \\frac{\\text{dollars}}{\\text{points}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, $\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}p}$ is measured in \"dollars per point\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa38f112-466c-50e9-ac08-be72b6f53782" :knowledge-point/title "Relating a Derivative to a Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dbef5d8-0793-57d1-8f7f-fdf923d5f6fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B'(t)=-(r(t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273297} {:answer/id #uuid "47d27bbb-1979-5f1b-a44d-6f50df53b362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B'(t)=r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273298} {:answer/id #uuid "c2422730-5ef9-5eb8-bc5e-8daf6f83c89b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B'(t)=\\frac{1}{2}(r(t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273299} {:answer/id #uuid "95912eed-be47-5c75-aefa-0b06533afa89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B'(t)=3(r(t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273300} {:answer/id #uuid "aac719d1-fb1a-551f-a52f-5223766fc160" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B'(t)=2(r(t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47d27bbb-1979-5f1b-a44d-6f50df53b362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B'(t)=r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273298} :answer-field/id #uuid "711a5de7-b52d-560b-a285-8047fd1aac6f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274943}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aa386819-df2b-519d-bbb5-a033b05e50c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-72109" :question/problem "Ramin, a bricklayer, is monitoring the construction of a wall by recording the following two numbers for different values of $t,$ where $t$ is the time, in hours, since he started his observations:\n\n- $B(t)$ - the number of bricks in the wall.\n\n- $r(t)$ - the rate at which the number of bricks in the wall is increasing.\n\nWhat is the relationship between $B(t)$ and $r(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $B(t)$ gives the number of bricks inside the wall after $t$ hours. Then,\n\n$$\n{B}^{'}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}B(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }B}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${B}^{'}(t)$ gives the rate at which the number of bricks increases at time $t$, which is the same as $r(t).$ Therefore, the relationship between $B(t)$ and $r(t)$ is ${B}^{'}(t)=r(t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91bf0b47-327f-5b82-a87a-307b7eda9e1c" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Arithmetic Series Using Sigma Notation when Not All Terms are Shown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01d23a8a-76ac-5d74-ab59-c7612ead8d56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{12}(6-\\frac{1}{2}n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273412} {:answer/id #uuid "2cb5b6bc-fe81-5444-80a1-6bf569d6abe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{12}(6-\\frac{1}{2}n)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273413} {:answer/id #uuid "4825f869-f279-5942-aaaa-115bb0d5f7b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{12}(6-\\frac{1}{2}n)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273414} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1780ea-d561-55cc-8b51-9bc0e4c5a4e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac12\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{12}(6-\\frac{1}{2}n)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273415} {:answer/id #uuid "ad93a3e8-a687-50a9-b898-9d260c5a9573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{12}(6-\\frac{1}{2}n)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01d23a8a-76ac-5d74-ab59-c7612ead8d56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{12}(6-\\frac{1}{2}n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273412} :answer-field/id #uuid "db94c850-9fc0-5d85-bc49-11bbe128e14e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274966}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "06651c53-f1bd-5c95-9ed0-46ecf11e946a" :question/math-academy-id "q-73302" :question/problem "Express the following arithmetic series using sigma notation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{11}{2}+5+\\frac{9}{2}+…+0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term ${a}_{1}=\\frac{11}{2}$ and the common difference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =5-\\frac{11}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the formula for the $n$th term is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2}+(n-1)(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}n+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =6-\\frac{1}{2}n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe don't know the index of the last term, but we can find it using the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =0 \\\\ 6-\\frac{1}{2}n & =0 \\\\ 6 & =\\frac{1}{2}n \\\\ 12 & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are $12$ terms in total, from $n=1$ to $n=12.$ So, the sigma notation for the given sum is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{12}(6-\\frac{1}{2}n).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261498 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e551c82-6984-5246-9e74-73fa3b55ffe1" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Arithmetic Series Using Sigma Notation Given Two Terms of the Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b549b1d1-78f9-5020-9ea3-8f2e77ae687e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(-2n+5)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273417} {:answer/id #uuid "1e59eb15-d761-5007-b1ab-9176d82af26e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(-2n+5)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273418} {:answer/id #uuid "25e538bf-b325-5a96-83ec-858731c14393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(-2n+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273419} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb2794d-1a0f-5480-8b99-eb09278e3158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(-2n+5)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273420} {:answer/id #uuid "f46ed132-1fc4-579c-8d01-3e54e1a04332" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac12\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(-2n+5)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25e538bf-b325-5a96-83ec-858731c14393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(-2n+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273419} :answer-field/id #uuid "42d15b25-09ca-587e-94ca-ff64e2de5bc8" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274967}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "342b667a-994a-57b7-a61a-0660abedb286" :question/math-academy-id "q-73303" :question/problem "An arithmetic series has ${a}_{1}=3$ and ${a}_{5}=-5,$ where ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{5}$ are the first and fifth terms of the series, respectively. What is the sum of the first ten terms of the series, expressed using sigma notation?" :question/worked-solution "We know that ${a}_{5}=-5.$ So, using the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\ -5 & =3+(5-1)d \\\\ -5 & =3+4d \\\\ 4d & =-8 \\\\ d & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${a}_{1}=3$ and $d=-2.$ Thus, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =3+(n-1)(-2) \\\\  & =3-2n+2 \\\\  & =-2n+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, the sum of the first ten terms can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(-2n+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7af2711d-f95d-549f-bb38-253383672e2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273746} {:answer/id #uuid "edbb695c-c1d7-5628-94de-c5ba3332c14a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273747} {:answer/id #uuid "54e65c67-4597-5f9d-8bf3-d5731b3a515a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273748} {:answer/id #uuid "a2859caa-cbfe-5529-b381-632005f10261" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7af2711d-f95d-549f-bb38-253383672e2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273746} :answer-field/id #uuid "743f0c7e-371b-5e72-92f2-fbf493e47feb" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275033}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94f25a88-826e-5f93-a9c5-c2932f446753" :question/math-academy-id "q-80680" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+3x+4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\nNote that the equation ${x}^{2}+3x+4=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={3}^{2}-4(1)(4) \\\\  & =9-16 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6532d0fb-1a24-5177-bd20-2efcb3e13f3d" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Geometric Series With a Negative Common Ratio Using Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f0f04de-7f2f-5177-8843-bae8320d8e21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{2}(-2)^{n}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274003} {:answer/id #uuid "6a50d0fd-2f14-57f3-b62a-a02f6bb8e90d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{2}(-2)^{n}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274004} {:answer/id #uuid "60bfd256-c695-56e0-9074-a9e0c07297c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{2}(-2)^{n}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274005} {:answer/id #uuid "41086d05-c2fe-5d0a-8e75-220d419c94c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{2}(-2)^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274006} {:answer/id #uuid "bffb2064-2311-5fcd-a16b-d9519554fffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac12\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{2}(-2)^{n}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41086d05-c2fe-5d0a-8e75-220d419c94c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{2}(-2)^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274006} :answer-field/id #uuid "e078599b-52dd-5f31-9b6f-09f029dd9683" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275085}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1790c68-9a6b-5d0b-85dc-5ef421b499cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-9755" :question/problem "The geometric series $-1+2-4+8-16+32\\,$ can be expressed using sigma notation as" :question/worked-solution "First, we must express the given series in the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ denotes the first term of the series, $r$ denotes the common ratio, and $N$ denotes the number of terms in the series. In this case, ${a}_{1}=-1$ and $N=6.$\n\nTo find $r,$ we calculate the ratio of the first two terms in the series:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2\n$$\n\nHence, expressing the geometric series given in sigma notation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n-1+2-4+8-16+32={\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}(-1)\\cdot (-2)^{n-1}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nFinally, we can simplify the above using the laws of exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}(-1)\\cdot (-2)^{n-1} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}(-1)\\cdot (-2)^{n}\\cdot (-2)^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}{2}^{-1}\\cdot (-2)^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{2}(-2)^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57e44759-4db8-57e3-bfb4-ce026d4d73b2" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Geometric Series Using Sigma Notation by Calculating the Number of Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c62923d-d951-5376-8fdd-da63e5487d0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac12\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}{4}^{n-1}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274008} {:answer/id #uuid "bdda068c-3b50-5d04-a3c3-63377e0c1983" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}{4}^{n-1}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274009} {:answer/id #uuid "8aa0f2ac-59cc-56ad-b093-f267a33d7765" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}{4}^{n-1}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274010} {:answer/id #uuid "30d16814-354f-52bf-9e30-d70158c9c14c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}{4}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274011} {:answer/id #uuid "f66c455b-e60d-5550-8b51-01a4ebd148a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}{4}^{n-1}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30d16814-354f-52bf-9e30-d70158c9c14c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}{4}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274011} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b1c255d-8095-5114-8e43-5640fec86eea" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275086}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2509ffde-6c9d-5b43-bb99-17cd7228a183" :question/math-academy-id "q-9760" :question/problem "Write the geometric series $1+4+16+⋯+4\\,096$ using sigma notation.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that $4\\,096={4}^{6}.$*" :question/worked-solution "We must express the given series in the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ denotes the first term of the series, $r$ denotes the common ratio, and $N$ denotes the number of terms in the series. In this case, ${a}_{1}=1.$\n\nTo find $r,$ we find the ratio of the consecutive terms ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{4}{1}=4\n$$\n\nNow we must determine $N$ (the index of the last term). To do this, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n{a}_{N}={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{N-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{N} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{N-1} \\\\ 4\\,096 & ={4}^{N-1} \\\\ {4}^{6} & ={4}^{N-1} \\\\ 6 & =N-1 \\\\ N & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing the geometric series in sigma notation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+4+16+⋯+4\\,096 & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}1\\cdot {4}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}{4}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
